WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:07.600 line:90%
Hi, I'm Roger Ross Williams.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.710 line:84%
I'm an African-American
man with dreadlocks

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:15.700 line:84%
all kind of bunched up in a
little band on top of my head.

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:19.750 line:84%
I am in Brooklyn, New
York in my office.

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:23.110 line:84%
And behind me is a
bookshelf with some sort

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:25.250 line:90%
of obnoxious awards.

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:27.770 line:90%
And it's great to be here.

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:31.600 line:84%
Hi, I'm Jim LeBrecht I'm
a 64-year-old white man.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:36.220 line:84%
I've got longish curly
hair, graying goatee,

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:38.590 line:90%
and I've got glasses on.

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:42.820 line:84%
I'm sitting in my home office,
which is in the back behind me

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:47.590 line:84%
is a red wall with some
music and movie posters.

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:48.910 line:90%
Hi, I'm Bao Nguyen.

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:51.940 line:84%
I'm a 36-year-old
Asian-American man

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:56.710 line:84%
with black hair
and a black beard.

00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:59.020 line:90%
I'm wearing a black polo.

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:00.880 line:90%
I'm in my studio in Los Angeles.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:08.050 line:84%
And behind me are a large bay of
windows, a sofa, and some books

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:10.030 line:90%
and posters.

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:12.010 line:90%
Hi, I'm Dawn Porter.

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:16.930 line:84%
I am a medium
brown-skinned woman

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:19.960 line:90%
of a certain age in my prime.

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:27.160 line:84%
I am upstairs in my home office
with a very cute modern chair

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:28.810 line:90%
next to me with a red cushion.

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:34.180 line:84%
I have on a kind of
pink plaid shirt.

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:39.760 line:84%
And my hair is short
and curly natural.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:42.490 line:90%
Hi, my name is Lisa Cortés.

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:45.580 line:90%
I am an Afro-Latina.

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:50.530 line:84%
My skin is cocoa brown,
kissed by the sun.

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:52.390 line:90%
I have dreadlocks.

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:57.430 line:84%
I am wearing white
glasses, a black dress,

00:01:57.430 --> 00:02:00.400 line:84%
with a blue and green
and gold necklace.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.950 line:90%
The walls behind me are red.

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:08.669 line:84%
And I am speaking from
Sugar Hill in Harlem.

00:02:08.669 --> 00:02:11.400 line:84%
Welcome to the next episode
of Academy Dialogues--

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:13.470 line:84%
It Starts with
Us, our continuing

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:17.040 line:84%
series of conversations about
race, ethnicity, gender,

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:20.190 line:84%
history, opportunity,
and the art of filmmaking

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:22.127 line:90%
hosted by the Academy.

00:02:22.127 --> 00:02:24.210 line:84%
My name is [? Shawn ?]
[? Finney, ?] and our topic

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:26.730 line:84%
today is documentaries
through our lens.

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:29.460 line:84%
We're honored to have five
highly awarded documentary

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:32.640 line:84%
filmmakers join us for this
discussion about the challenges

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.710 line:84%
people from
underrepresented communities

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:37.410 line:84%
still face in the
documentary world.

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:40.890 line:84%
Our moderator is the prolific
filmmaker and Academy governor,

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:42.990 line:90%
Roger Ross Williams.

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:46.320 line:84%
Roger is an award-winning
director, producer, writer,

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:49.350 line:84%
and became the first
African-American director

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:54.780 line:84%
to win an Academy Award with his
short film Music by Prudence.

00:02:54.780 --> 00:03:00.600 line:84%
Joining Roger will be
director producer Lisa Cortés,

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:06.630 line:84%
filmmaker Jim LeBrecht, director
and producer Bao Nguyen,

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:09.150 line:90%
and filmmaker Dawn Porter.

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:11.730 line:84%
We look forward to
your conversation.

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:13.330 line:90%
Welcome, everybody.

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:17.010 line:84%
Thank you for coming
today and participating

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:19.710 line:90%
in this in the dialogues.

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:23.260 line:90%
I wanted to start--

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:27.420 line:84%
so the conversation about
who gets to tell the story

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:28.510 line:90%
is not a new one.

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:30.300 line:90%
We all know that.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:36.780 line:84%
We've traditionally been defined
through other people's lenses.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:39.900 line:84%
The documentary industry
has been seen as a whole

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:45.390 line:84%
as progressive, as someone as
a community that is actually

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:48.580 line:84%
ahead of the game in the
entertainment business.

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:50.250 line:84%
But I think the
dirty little secret

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:55.830 line:84%
is the documentary community
is not that ahead of the game

00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:58.960 line:84%
and that we have a
lot of work to do.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:03.270 line:84%
I wanted to start by sort
of taking your temperature

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:07.920 line:84%
about kind of the state
of documentary for people

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:09.930 line:84%
from underrepresented
communities

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:12.570 line:84%
and where you think
we are right now.

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:14.130 line:84%
And I want to--
and since you all

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:17.079 line:84%
are coming from
different perspectives,

00:04:17.079 --> 00:04:20.170 line:84%
and I'm going to start with
the elder statesman here, Dawn

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:20.670 line:90%
Porter.

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:29.430 line:84%
Well, it's a joy, a privilege
to be here with all of you

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:32.520 line:84%
whose work I respect so
much, and I appreciate you

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:33.930 line:90%
all being here.

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:37.590 line:84%
And of course, the
greatest admiration,

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:41.520 line:84%
love, and respect, and
honor to my governor.

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:49.080 line:84%
So I think that we are at a
very strange time, coming off

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:55.110 line:84%
of the idea and
the common wisdom

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:57.730 line:84%
that this is the golden
age of documentary.

00:04:57.730 --> 00:05:00.960 line:84%
And there's more work than
ever, and there's more appetite

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:02.100 line:90%
and audience appetite.

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:04.950 line:84%
And there's particularly
an appetite for stories

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:07.620 line:90%
about underrepresented people.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:11.160 line:84%
I think that's a
testament just to how

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:14.040 line:84%
broad and vast
the audience likes

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:18.780 line:90%
to see themselves on screen.

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:24.210 line:84%
And yet, when I think
about who the creators are.

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:27.010 line:84%
They are being asked
to tell those stories.

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:31.470 line:84%
I do not see that demand
meeting this supply.

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:36.270 line:84%
And I mean that
by I have the kind

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:42.840 line:84%
of feeling that while there is
demand for stories about people

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:45.210 line:84%
from underrepresented
communities,

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:49.620 line:84%
I don't see the gatekeepers
or people with the funds

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:51.540 line:90%
to make those stories happening.

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:56.010 line:84%
I don't see a really
wide net being cast.

00:05:56.010 --> 00:05:58.900 line:84%
And I think that
that's a big problem.

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:02.430 line:90%
And one thing--

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:06.060 line:84%
I think this conversation is
necessary, but it's also hard.

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:10.890 line:84%
And it's hard because
the documentary-- there

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:12.600 line:90%
is a community here.

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:16.500 line:84%
And we do work together,
or we appear in panels,

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:17.740 line:90%
or we support each other.

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:19.240 line:90%
We help each other.

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:24.690 line:84%
And so I think people have been
a little reticent to criticize

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:30.420 line:84%
when filmmakers take on
projects that maybe there's

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:33.260 line:84%
another voice that's better
suited to take on that project

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:35.760 line:90%
so I welcome this conversation.

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:38.340 line:84%
I'm going to be honest
about my feelings about it.

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:44.190 line:84%
But I think we need to push
the people who can really

00:06:44.190 --> 00:06:47.910 line:84%
greenlight and make films
happen to look beyond the very

00:06:47.910 --> 00:06:50.190 line:84%
narrow group of
filmmakers that they

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:53.000 line:84%
are looking to
tell these stories

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:55.056 line:90%
if there's a demand for.

00:06:55.056 --> 00:06:55.556 line:90%
Yeah.

00:06:55.556 --> 00:06:56.550 line:90%
Yeah.

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:57.300 line:90%
Absolutely.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:58.290 line:90%
Absolutely.

00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:04.740 line:84%
Bao, you're new,
relatively young filmmaker.

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:10.550 line:84%
And I just want to talk to
you about your perspective

00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:14.040 line:90%
as someone who is--

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:16.800 line:84%
I guess Be Water is
your first film--

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:20.580 line:84%
someone sort of starting out
in the doc industry and what

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:22.340 line:90%
your observations are.

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:25.620 line:84%
Well, I just wanted to
also express my gratitude

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:28.380 line:84%
for being invited to
this really amazing panel

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:29.520 line:90%
of fellow filmmakers.

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:31.860 line:90%
I really respect as well.

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:34.080 line:84%
Be Water is actually
my second film,

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:38.490 line:84%
but my first film
was not in the way

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:40.800 line:84%
that I really wanted
to tell that story.

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:45.330 line:84%
And I think a lot of
times as filmmakers

00:07:45.330 --> 00:07:50.400 line:84%
we feel like it's necessarily to
tell it to a certain audience.

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:53.610 line:84%
We're often asked like oh,
how does this story relate

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:56.490 line:84%
to a Western audience,
to an American audience,

00:07:56.490 --> 00:07:58.170 line:90%
to a white audience?

00:07:58.170 --> 00:08:03.230 line:84%
And some of that
really kind of I think

00:08:03.230 --> 00:08:07.280 line:84%
filters our own voice in a
way that feels inauthentic,

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:08.720 line:90%
and it's not honest.

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:11.420 line:84%
And what I found with
making Be Water which

00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:13.670 line:84%
I was so fortunate
and privileged to make

00:08:13.670 --> 00:08:16.250 line:84%
was that I could tell a story
that was unapologetically

00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:18.500 line:84%
Asian-American, that
was unapologetically

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:22.280 line:84%
about Bruce Lee's struggle as
an Asian-American in Hollywood.

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:25.370 line:84%
And I think that
is shifting a bit.

00:08:25.370 --> 00:08:30.290 line:84%
But I recognize that it sort of
becomes a trend in the industry

00:08:30.290 --> 00:08:33.770 line:84%
that we can have
these periods of OK,

00:08:33.770 --> 00:08:37.182 line:84%
we're going to have films
that are very authentic,

00:08:37.182 --> 00:08:39.640 line:84%
and then we're going to pat
ourselves on the back about it.

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:43.039 line:84%
But is it something that's
sustainable over time?

00:08:43.039 --> 00:08:47.120 line:84%
And I think learning
from Bruce Lee, actually,

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:50.630 line:84%
I understood what it meant to
be authentic as a filmmaker,

00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:51.880 line:90%
as a storyteller.

00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:54.290 line:84%
I've used those
lessons to sort of try

00:08:54.290 --> 00:08:56.960 line:84%
to push myself for
as a filmmaker,

00:08:56.960 --> 00:09:00.050 line:84%
as a documentary filmmaker,
and not think so much of oh,

00:09:00.050 --> 00:09:02.600 line:84%
how does this connect
to the Western audience?

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:04.790 line:84%
How does this connect to
a white American audience?

00:09:04.790 --> 00:09:08.420 line:84%
It's like I should think of
myself as an audience member

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:09.920 line:90%
that matters as well, right?

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:12.320 line:84%
And I think we are
often told, in terms

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:15.467 line:84%
of these spaces for
documentary filmmakers--

00:09:15.467 --> 00:09:16.550 line:90%
especially in development.

00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:21.230 line:84%
It's really hard to get films
developed, because we are--

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:26.300 line:84%
I think many BIPOC,
marginalized filmmakers,

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:27.890 line:84%
they're not seen
for their potential.

00:09:27.890 --> 00:09:31.040 line:84%
We have to live up to a
higher threshold of success

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:34.430 line:84%
than a lot of other
filmmakers out there who

00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:36.920 line:84%
personally have the
same experience in terms

00:09:36.920 --> 00:09:39.505 line:90%
of work experience.

00:09:39.505 --> 00:09:40.880 line:84%
But I think we
have to also think

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:43.100 line:84%
about the value
of our experience

00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:46.700 line:84%
as marginalized communities,
as marginalized individuals,

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:49.430 line:84%
and not think about the
price of representation

00:09:49.430 --> 00:09:52.350 line:84%
but the value of
representation more.

00:09:52.350 --> 00:09:53.110 line:90%
Yeah.

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:54.000 line:90%
Absolutely.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:55.560 line:90%
Absolutely.

00:09:55.560 --> 00:10:00.300 line:84%
Lisa, you came from
the scripted world--

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:02.610 line:90%
produced Precious.

00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:08.940 line:84%
And your second documentary
All In: The Fight for Democracy

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:11.205 line:90%
is dropping today or tomorrow.

00:10:14.350 --> 00:10:16.260 line:84%
Do you see a difference
in the doc community

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:18.000 line:90%
from the scripted community?

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:20.260 line:90%
And what is that?

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:29.845 line:84%
Well, I'm not different in it
in how I operate in either.

00:10:32.870 --> 00:10:38.570 line:84%
I love what Bao said about being
unapologetic in your voice,

00:10:38.570 --> 00:10:41.030 line:90%
in your vision.

00:10:41.030 --> 00:10:45.740 line:84%
And oftentimes, what
we're trying to figure out

00:10:45.740 --> 00:10:49.820 line:84%
is how did we arrive at this
moment and with this story.

00:10:49.820 --> 00:10:58.070 line:84%
And for myself, I am
looking to center the value

00:10:58.070 --> 00:11:07.550 line:84%
of my experience, and what
it brings to the project,

00:11:07.550 --> 00:11:11.930 line:84%
And my responsibility
to my community.

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:15.410 line:84%
So whether it's
scripted or narrative,

00:11:15.410 --> 00:11:22.600 line:84%
I kind of-- my north star
as a creator doesn't change.

00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:29.690 line:84%
Jim, I feel like often
differently abled people

00:11:29.690 --> 00:11:30.440 line:90%
are left--

00:11:30.440 --> 00:11:32.900 line:84%
especially definitely
filmmakers are left--

00:11:32.900 --> 00:11:34.160 line:90%
out of the conversation.

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:40.470 line:84%
I've been on many
diversity panels.

00:11:40.470 --> 00:11:43.340 line:84%
And when we were
talking about diversity,

00:11:43.340 --> 00:11:46.730 line:84%
we often leave out
people with disabilities.

00:11:49.790 --> 00:11:53.610 line:84%
I made a film about a young
woman with a disability.

00:11:53.610 --> 00:12:01.040 line:84%
And my eyes were open to my
own sort of bias, my own--

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:03.410 line:90%
being uncomfortable.

00:12:03.410 --> 00:12:06.980 line:84%
And it was a huge
learning curve to learn,

00:12:06.980 --> 00:12:12.320 line:84%
to exist in that
community and understand

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:15.800 line:84%
who they are as they were
all young people in Africa.

00:12:18.530 --> 00:12:26.450 line:84%
I want to ask you to talk a
little bit about that and about

00:12:26.450 --> 00:12:31.160 line:84%
in the documentary community,
about what voices like yours,

00:12:31.160 --> 00:12:34.760 line:84%
and why we're not hearing
from a lot of people

00:12:34.760 --> 00:12:36.663 line:90%
with disabilities.

00:12:36.663 --> 00:12:38.080 line:84%
Well, thank you
for that question.

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:43.350 line:84%
And I also want to echo
how absolutely honored I am

00:12:43.350 --> 00:12:48.900 line:84%
to be with all of you today and
to be part of this discussion.

00:12:48.900 --> 00:12:52.620 line:84%
And to be honest with
you, I think that just

00:12:52.620 --> 00:12:57.570 line:84%
my presence here today is
kind of what the issue is that

00:12:57.570 --> 00:13:02.490 line:84%
just getting in the door,
simply being in the room

00:13:02.490 --> 00:13:05.310 line:84%
has been difficult for a lot
of people with disabilities.

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:09.990 line:90%
And indeed, I was seeing--

00:13:09.990 --> 00:13:11.460 line:84%
just a few years
ago, I was seeing

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:16.950 line:84%
all these different diversity
and inclusion movements,

00:13:16.950 --> 00:13:22.170 line:84%
including at the IDA, the
International Documentary

00:13:22.170 --> 00:13:23.790 line:90%
Association.

00:13:23.790 --> 00:13:27.450 line:84%
And when they had
Getting Real in 2016,

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:31.170 line:84%
the convention was all about
kind of inclusion stuff,

00:13:31.170 --> 00:13:34.270 line:84%
and there wasn't anything
about disability.

00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:40.740 line:84%
But I've seen a lot of
really positive change.

00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:45.450 line:84%
And the IDA really embraced
that and really addressed that.

00:13:45.450 --> 00:13:50.970 line:84%
And when they had Getting
Real two years ago,

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:54.490 line:84%
we had panels on
disabled filmmakers.

00:13:54.490 --> 00:14:00.690 line:84%
And we had a convening of
disabled filmmakers and allies.

00:14:00.690 --> 00:14:03.330 line:84%
And that was the first
time that people actually--

00:14:03.330 --> 00:14:06.960 line:84%
different filmmakers, all sorts
of different disabilities,

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:09.300 line:84%
or if they were dead
or hard of hearing,

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:12.610 line:84%
were in the same
room with each other.

00:14:12.610 --> 00:14:15.780 line:84%
So I think that
people are finally

00:14:15.780 --> 00:14:20.400 line:84%
starting to recognize that we
are a marginalized community,

00:14:20.400 --> 00:14:22.050 line:84%
and that what we
are dealing with

00:14:22.050 --> 00:14:24.930 line:84%
is a civil rights
issue in our community.

00:14:24.930 --> 00:14:28.650 line:84%
That it's not purely like
medical, these people are sick,

00:14:28.650 --> 00:14:34.710 line:84%
but that really this is
about being part of society.

00:14:34.710 --> 00:14:39.210 line:84%
So I'm more optimistic though
than I was a couple years ago.

00:14:39.210 --> 00:14:40.160 line:90%
Great.

00:14:40.160 --> 00:14:43.350 line:84%
You used-- you talked
about "diversity"

00:14:43.350 --> 00:14:45.740 line:84%
and "inclusiveness"--
those words.

00:14:45.740 --> 00:14:49.860 line:84%
And I want to talk a little
bit about those terms.

00:14:49.860 --> 00:14:52.740 line:84%
And how many how
many panels have we

00:14:52.740 --> 00:14:58.680 line:84%
seen about diversity, about
throwing around these terms.

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:02.220 line:84%
There's every now with this sort
of racial reckoning happening

00:15:02.220 --> 00:15:04.380 line:90%
in America.

00:15:04.380 --> 00:15:07.170 line:84%
Every corporation, the
entertainment industry,

00:15:07.170 --> 00:15:11.490 line:84%
everyone is jumping on
this sort of diversity,

00:15:11.490 --> 00:15:14.430 line:84%
using the word "diversity,"
and jumping on this bandwagon.

00:15:14.430 --> 00:15:25.180 line:84%
For me it feels like that
diversity means assimilation.

00:15:25.180 --> 00:15:29.070 line:90%
It means we have to fit within--

00:15:29.070 --> 00:15:34.290 line:84%
they want a person of color,
a person with a disability.

00:15:34.290 --> 00:15:36.540 line:84%
They want them in
the room but just

00:15:36.540 --> 00:15:42.960 line:84%
to reinforce their own ideology,
their own way that they've

00:15:42.960 --> 00:15:44.340 line:90%
always done things--

00:15:44.340 --> 00:15:49.540 line:84%
not really opening the door
to our voices and our stories.

00:15:49.540 --> 00:15:51.630 line:84%
So I would just
want to go around.

00:15:51.630 --> 00:15:55.590 line:84%
And I wanted to ask
each one of you, how

00:15:55.590 --> 00:15:59.070 line:84%
do you define "diversity,"
"inclusion," these terms?

00:15:59.070 --> 00:16:00.420 line:90%
How do you define "diversity"?

00:16:00.420 --> 00:16:02.280 line:90%
And what does it mean to you?

00:16:02.280 --> 00:16:06.960 line:84%
And I'll start with
you, Dawn Porter.

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:13.300 line:84%
I think there's-- in
the black community,

00:16:13.300 --> 00:16:17.400 line:84%
we always say like we don't
usually have the opportunity

00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:22.140 line:84%
to hide our difference, because
I wear my difference proudly

00:16:22.140 --> 00:16:23.970 line:90%
and happily.

00:16:23.970 --> 00:16:27.070 line:90%
But I think that there are--

00:16:27.070 --> 00:16:32.250 line:84%
I'm very concerned about
people who are not wealthy,

00:16:32.250 --> 00:16:36.270 line:84%
people from other areas who
are not from New York City

00:16:36.270 --> 00:16:40.487 line:84%
or from Los Angeles
or San Francisco,

00:16:40.487 --> 00:16:42.070 line:84%
people from the
middle of the country.

00:16:42.070 --> 00:16:47.410 line:90%
I feel like there is a--

00:16:47.410 --> 00:16:50.240 line:84%
the reason that we
want authentic voices

00:16:50.240 --> 00:16:54.400 line:84%
is because we want to do
something approximating truth.

00:16:54.400 --> 00:16:59.490 line:84%
And so I really love flat
out saying about whatever

00:16:59.490 --> 00:17:00.900 line:90%
our differences are.

00:17:00.900 --> 00:17:06.089 line:84%
And that begs the question
is, what are we comparing to?

00:17:06.089 --> 00:17:09.660 line:84%
But whatever our
individual experiences are,

00:17:09.660 --> 00:17:11.220 line:90%
that is a gift.

00:17:11.220 --> 00:17:14.430 line:84%
That is what we
want to encourage.

00:17:14.430 --> 00:17:21.510 line:84%
So I do really try and think
hard about economic diversity,

00:17:21.510 --> 00:17:26.980 line:84%
geographic diversity,
and life experience.

00:17:26.980 --> 00:17:32.250 line:84%
No matter what race you are,
if you come from a really

00:17:32.250 --> 00:17:35.130 line:84%
kind of difficult
financial circumstance,

00:17:35.130 --> 00:17:39.560 line:84%
I think you do have a different
perspective on issues.

00:17:39.560 --> 00:17:41.770 line:84%
And I think that
those are voices that

00:17:41.770 --> 00:17:45.310 line:90%
really should be encouraged.

00:17:45.310 --> 00:17:45.840 line:90%
Yeah.

00:17:45.840 --> 00:17:48.100 line:90%
Yeah.

00:17:48.100 --> 00:17:50.750 line:90%
Jim?

00:17:50.750 --> 00:17:55.670 line:84%
I often think about this
as who is not in the room

00:17:55.670 --> 00:17:58.460 line:84%
if I'm really looking
about inclusion.

00:17:58.460 --> 00:18:05.270 line:84%
And indeed, what Dawn has talked
about here is really important.

00:18:05.270 --> 00:18:09.170 line:84%
I never thought about people
who were simply geographically

00:18:09.170 --> 00:18:12.965 line:84%
marginalized and
their ability to be

00:18:12.965 --> 00:18:14.090 line:90%
able to tell their stories.

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:20.790 line:84%
And even within the
disabled community,

00:18:20.790 --> 00:18:23.130 line:90%
there's such a wide umbrella.

00:18:23.130 --> 00:18:24.760 line:84%
There are people with
cognitive issues.

00:18:24.760 --> 00:18:27.470 line:84%
There are people with
mental health issues

00:18:27.470 --> 00:18:29.030 line:90%
that they're dealing with.

00:18:29.030 --> 00:18:32.510 line:84%
And that it's important
for us to make sure

00:18:32.510 --> 00:18:34.610 line:84%
that we are really
not leaving anybody

00:18:34.610 --> 00:18:40.380 line:84%
behind in regards to how we
are looking out for each other.

00:18:40.380 --> 00:18:43.170 line:84%
And I think that
for me, personally,

00:18:43.170 --> 00:18:46.430 line:84%
that obviously goes well
beyond just disability.

00:18:46.430 --> 00:18:48.870 line:84%
But that my experience
performs how

00:18:48.870 --> 00:18:51.430 line:84%
I look at anybody
that I'm working with

00:18:51.430 --> 00:18:55.434 line:84%
and when I check and see
who's not in the room.

00:18:55.434 --> 00:18:55.934 line:90%
Yeah.

00:18:59.266 --> 00:19:02.380 line:84%
Yeah, I mean it's like
what Angela Davis said.

00:19:02.380 --> 00:19:04.150 line:84%
Diversity is a
corporate strategy,

00:19:04.150 --> 00:19:07.080 line:84%
and I feel like it's becoming
more and more that way

00:19:07.080 --> 00:19:08.370 line:90%
in many conversations.

00:19:08.370 --> 00:19:11.860 line:84%
And I mean, I appreciate
that this conversation is--

00:19:11.860 --> 00:19:15.700 line:84%
the word "diversity" is part
of the panel title, right?

00:19:15.700 --> 00:19:16.360 line:90%
And

00:19:16.360 --> 00:19:18.130 line:84%
I think it's all
about like how we

00:19:18.130 --> 00:19:20.717 line:90%
center anything in the world.

00:19:20.717 --> 00:19:21.550 line:90%
What's the standard?

00:19:21.550 --> 00:19:22.780 line:90%
What's considered normal?

00:19:22.780 --> 00:19:25.630 line:84%
And when we diverged
from that, that's what

00:19:25.630 --> 00:19:27.700 line:90%
diversity is considered, right?

00:19:27.700 --> 00:19:29.230 line:84%
Who determines
what the center is,

00:19:29.230 --> 00:19:31.660 line:84%
what the center of
our narratives are,

00:19:31.660 --> 00:19:32.410 line:90%
what the center--

00:19:32.410 --> 00:19:34.300 line:90%
what the gold standard is?

00:19:34.300 --> 00:19:36.490 line:90%
What's the center of merit?

00:19:36.490 --> 00:19:37.750 line:90%
What's the center of success?

00:19:37.750 --> 00:19:41.230 line:84%
And even thinking
geographically,

00:19:41.230 --> 00:19:44.110 line:84%
Western cinema, Western
films is considered

00:19:44.110 --> 00:19:45.610 line:90%
the gold standard of success.

00:19:45.610 --> 00:19:48.490 line:84%
But I think even this
past year with Parasite

00:19:48.490 --> 00:19:50.390 line:84%
winning, I thought
it's very encouraging,

00:19:50.390 --> 00:19:53.890 line:84%
because we're recentering what
the best picture is in a way.

00:19:53.890 --> 00:19:56.260 line:84%
And when you think of
that, it just opens up

00:19:56.260 --> 00:19:57.040 line:90%
the opportunities.

00:19:57.040 --> 00:20:01.240 line:84%
It opens up our borders of not
just thinking about the West

00:20:01.240 --> 00:20:03.080 line:84%
as being the center
of the world.

00:20:03.080 --> 00:20:09.670 line:84%
And I think given how global
or how globally we're connected

00:20:09.670 --> 00:20:11.890 line:84%
and trying to build
community, especially

00:20:11.890 --> 00:20:15.010 line:84%
through film, documentaries
in a way I think

00:20:15.010 --> 00:20:17.230 line:84%
have been a bit more
progressive in that.

00:20:17.230 --> 00:20:20.380 line:84%
If you look at the best
documentary nominations

00:20:20.380 --> 00:20:23.380 line:84%
last year, Honeyland,
For Sarma, I

00:20:23.380 --> 00:20:27.490 line:84%
think those opportunities
to rethink the center.

00:20:27.490 --> 00:20:29.258 line:84%
And how we're
centering narratives

00:20:29.258 --> 00:20:31.300 line:84%
is important when we're
thinking about diversity.

00:20:31.300 --> 00:20:36.190 line:84%
Because to me, a
white male is diverse.

00:20:36.190 --> 00:20:39.790 line:84%
Someone brought that up in
an earlier Academy Dialogue.

00:20:39.790 --> 00:20:41.200 line:90%
And I was like, that's perfect.

00:20:41.200 --> 00:20:42.430 line:90%
For me, I'm not diverse.

00:20:42.430 --> 00:20:44.110 line:90%
I am exists as who I am.

00:20:44.110 --> 00:20:47.952 line:84%
And Dawn was saying when
you're around your community,

00:20:47.952 --> 00:20:48.910 line:90%
you don't feel diverse.

00:20:48.910 --> 00:20:50.243 line:90%
You just feel like you're there.

00:20:50.243 --> 00:20:52.390 line:90%
You're allowed to be there.

00:20:52.390 --> 00:20:54.070 line:90%
You're allowed to exist.

00:20:54.070 --> 00:20:55.600 line:90%
So I think rethinking--

00:20:55.600 --> 00:20:58.420 line:84%
as we're reckoning a lot of
things in America in the world,

00:20:58.420 --> 00:21:02.340 line:84%
we have to also think of
what it means to be diverse.

00:21:02.340 --> 00:21:03.120 line:90%
Exactly.

00:21:03.120 --> 00:21:04.980 line:90%
I love that.

00:21:04.980 --> 00:21:06.120 line:90%
Most of the world--

00:21:06.120 --> 00:21:09.150 line:84%
when I was driving here, I
was thinking most of the world

00:21:09.150 --> 00:21:17.650 line:84%
is not white, European-centered
reality perspective.

00:21:17.650 --> 00:21:21.430 line:84%
And so what is this--
what does diversity mean?

00:21:21.430 --> 00:21:23.880 line:84%
It means that we're trying
to fit within that--

00:21:23.880 --> 00:21:26.460 line:84%
we're trying to-- the
types of films we make,

00:21:26.460 --> 00:21:29.250 line:84%
everything we do, we're trying
to-- are we trying to like fit

00:21:29.250 --> 00:21:31.770 line:84%
within that sort
of [? their ?] sort

00:21:31.770 --> 00:21:35.520 line:84%
of [? reality we're ?]
trying to assimilate.

00:21:35.520 --> 00:21:36.810 line:90%
But to be an--

00:21:36.810 --> 00:21:40.860 line:84%
to be an anti-racist, it's
not about assimilating

00:21:40.860 --> 00:21:43.080 line:90%
into anyone's reality.

00:21:43.080 --> 00:21:45.090 line:84%
It's about your own
communities like Dawn said.

00:21:45.090 --> 00:21:48.550 line:84%
It's about who you are
in your own community.

00:21:48.550 --> 00:21:50.910 line:84%
So I think that's
really important part

00:21:50.910 --> 00:21:54.480 line:90%
of the conversation here.

00:21:54.480 --> 00:21:57.060 line:90%
Lisa, Lisa Cortés.

00:21:57.060 --> 00:21:59.780 line:90%
Well, Roger, I think so many--

00:21:59.780 --> 00:22:04.670 line:84%
we've talked a lot about
diversity, inclusion,

00:22:04.670 --> 00:22:08.540 line:84%
but then measuring
it against progress.

00:22:08.540 --> 00:22:11.990 line:84%
Like who is getting
awarded the projects?

00:22:11.990 --> 00:22:15.230 line:84%
Who has the access
to the funding?

00:22:15.230 --> 00:22:20.180 line:84%
And I've been
dreaming of a world

00:22:20.180 --> 00:22:23.390 line:84%
where we can stop having
the conversations,

00:22:23.390 --> 00:22:27.680 line:84%
because we will see that
tangible progress has

00:22:27.680 --> 00:22:32.340 line:84%
been made in terms of
who's getting projects.

00:22:35.900 --> 00:22:39.680 line:84%
And more importantly,
that otherness

00:22:39.680 --> 00:22:44.840 line:84%
is not being used as
a way of diminishing

00:22:44.840 --> 00:22:51.110 line:84%
the value of what we were create
and the visibility of what

00:22:51.110 --> 00:22:51.740 line:90%
we create.

00:22:54.530 --> 00:22:58.700 line:84%
In this conversation, we
have to look at privilege.

00:22:58.700 --> 00:23:05.030 line:84%
Because that is certainly
one of the boundaries

00:23:05.030 --> 00:23:10.370 line:84%
that also comes in
to this conversation.

00:23:10.370 --> 00:23:18.200 line:84%
Whatever privilege decision
makers are is working from is--

00:23:18.200 --> 00:23:19.490 line:90%
we have all seen.

00:23:19.490 --> 00:23:23.300 line:84%
It has been a deterrent
that those gatekeepers

00:23:23.300 --> 00:23:25.530 line:90%
have introduced.

00:23:25.530 --> 00:23:28.790 line:90%
So this is an interesting time.

00:23:28.790 --> 00:23:31.280 line:90%
It's a time of reckoning.

00:23:31.280 --> 00:23:37.100 line:84%
And I think if we all want
to get to truth in our work,

00:23:37.100 --> 00:23:40.920 line:84%
in this business, and
in our storytelling,

00:23:40.920 --> 00:23:44.180 line:84%
we have to have these very
difficult conversations

00:23:44.180 --> 00:23:51.170 line:84%
and move beyond the
conversation to action.

00:23:51.170 --> 00:23:55.460 line:84%
There was something
that you said about--

00:23:55.460 --> 00:23:59.810 line:84%
as governor and as
someone who heads up

00:23:59.810 --> 00:24:04.760 line:84%
the documentary
diversity committee,

00:24:04.760 --> 00:24:08.450 line:84%
I've actually had
filmmakers complain to me

00:24:08.450 --> 00:24:13.040 line:84%
that good old sort
of American films

00:24:13.040 --> 00:24:19.650 line:84%
are not being nominated,
that too many foreign films--

00:24:19.650 --> 00:24:22.940 line:84%
that would sound familiar too
many foreign films are getting

00:24:22.940 --> 00:24:26.060 line:84%
nominated in the
documentary category,

00:24:26.060 --> 00:24:29.640 line:84%
that there were two films
from Syria last year.

00:24:29.640 --> 00:24:37.730 line:84%
And it just blows my mind that
the privilege, and that you

00:24:37.730 --> 00:24:42.860 line:84%
are entitled because you are an
American, which means you are--

00:24:42.860 --> 00:24:49.100 line:84%
sort of is a code word
for white filmmakers

00:24:49.100 --> 00:24:53.360 line:84%
who have dominated
the doc branch.

00:24:53.360 --> 00:24:57.470 line:84%
So that has been a big
sort of pet peeve of mine,

00:24:57.470 --> 00:24:59.708 line:90%
so I just had to say that.

00:25:02.520 --> 00:25:07.940 line:84%
How much-- you guys
are-- all of you--

00:25:07.940 --> 00:25:10.880 line:90%
are all really successful.

00:25:10.880 --> 00:25:18.350 line:84%
You all have major, major films
that are with big distributors.

00:25:18.350 --> 00:25:20.900 line:90%
And your names are known.

00:25:20.900 --> 00:25:22.430 line:90%
You're out there in the world.

00:25:22.430 --> 00:25:24.170 line:84%
How much responsibility
do you guys

00:25:24.170 --> 00:25:27.620 line:84%
feel to lift up
others like yourself?

00:25:27.620 --> 00:25:30.860 line:84%
So is the weight-- there's
so much weight on all of you

00:25:30.860 --> 00:25:33.530 line:90%
guys, because you're at the top.

00:25:33.530 --> 00:25:36.770 line:90%
So Jim talk to me about that.

00:25:36.770 --> 00:25:40.110 line:84%
Do you feel that sense
of responsibility?

00:25:40.110 --> 00:25:41.240 line:90%
I think it's--

00:25:41.240 --> 00:25:43.320 line:90%
I think it's really huge.

00:25:43.320 --> 00:25:50.660 line:84%
I think that my responsibility,
I feel, is to share the mic,

00:25:50.660 --> 00:25:55.010 line:84%
is to bring other people,
provide opportunities

00:25:55.010 --> 00:25:56.870 line:84%
for other people
within my community

00:25:56.870 --> 00:26:00.040 line:84%
and outside my
community that I know

00:26:00.040 --> 00:26:02.840 line:90%
that have not been represented.

00:26:02.840 --> 00:26:10.120 line:84%
And so it's and
also there are not

00:26:10.120 --> 00:26:14.330 line:84%
that many well-known
filmmakers with disabilities.

00:26:18.230 --> 00:26:22.790 line:84%
So I feel like boy, I
better not screw up.

00:26:26.170 --> 00:26:29.960 line:84%
But well beyond
that, it's just--

00:26:29.960 --> 00:26:32.720 line:84%
I'm looking forward to
the day where many of us

00:26:32.720 --> 00:26:36.980 line:90%
are known for our work.

00:26:36.980 --> 00:26:40.830 line:90%
And so that it's--

00:26:40.830 --> 00:26:42.950 line:84%
so I'm not being
terribly clear here,

00:26:42.950 --> 00:26:47.510 line:84%
but I think my point is
that when we are leaders,

00:26:47.510 --> 00:26:48.740 line:90%
it's really responsive.

00:26:48.740 --> 00:26:53.690 line:84%
It's important that we take
this very, very seriously

00:26:53.690 --> 00:26:55.510 line:90%
and try to help others.

00:26:55.510 --> 00:26:56.010 line:90%
Yeah.

00:26:56.010 --> 00:26:56.540 line:90%
Exactly.

00:26:56.540 --> 00:26:58.250 line:90%
Lisa, you said that--

00:26:58.250 --> 00:26:59.930 line:84%
you were quoted
as saying we don't

00:26:59.930 --> 00:27:04.430 line:90%
need an inclusivity writer.

00:27:04.430 --> 00:27:06.770 line:90%
I believe that was your quote.

00:27:06.770 --> 00:27:08.720 line:90%
Elaborate on that.

00:27:08.720 --> 00:27:12.920 line:84%
Well, I'd first like to jump in
and talk about your question,

00:27:12.920 --> 00:27:15.860 line:90%
Roger, about responsibility.

00:27:15.860 --> 00:27:18.500 line:84%
What my parents always
impressed upon me

00:27:18.500 --> 00:27:22.820 line:84%
is that responsibility
comes with accountability

00:27:22.820 --> 00:27:25.200 line:90%
to your community.

00:27:25.200 --> 00:27:30.020 line:84%
One of my favorite speeches is
from Dr. Martin Luther King,

00:27:30.020 --> 00:27:32.600 line:84%
and it's called the
"Drum Major Instinct."

00:27:32.600 --> 00:27:37.940 line:84%
And he says that
you can be a star.

00:27:37.940 --> 00:27:43.560 line:84%
You can be all these
wonderful things.

00:27:43.560 --> 00:27:49.760 line:84%
But if you cannot be of
service, then that is what is

00:27:49.760 --> 00:27:51.920 line:90%
your greatest responsibility.

00:27:51.920 --> 00:27:53.930 line:90%
That's my responsibility.

00:27:53.930 --> 00:27:58.230 line:84%
And I love it because he's like,
it doesn't matter if you can't

00:27:58.230 --> 00:27:59.480 line:90%
do all these wonderful things.

00:27:59.480 --> 00:28:03.650 line:84%
You can always be a
drum major for justice.

00:28:03.650 --> 00:28:06.740 line:84%
You can be a drum
major for peace.

00:28:06.740 --> 00:28:10.700 line:84%
You can be a drum major
for accountability.

00:28:10.700 --> 00:28:14.450 line:84%
And if people don't
know the speech,

00:28:14.450 --> 00:28:16.460 line:84%
I'm not doing it
service, because it's

00:28:16.460 --> 00:28:17.930 line:90%
so beautiful and eloquent.

00:28:17.930 --> 00:28:22.760 line:84%
But it is something that is
one of my important guiding

00:28:22.760 --> 00:28:24.800 line:90%
principles.

00:28:24.800 --> 00:28:30.050 line:84%
Now, when I made a
comment about like

00:28:30.050 --> 00:28:34.190 line:84%
we shouldn't need to
have inclusively writers.

00:28:34.190 --> 00:28:35.510 line:90%
We shouldn't need to.

00:28:35.510 --> 00:28:40.550 line:84%
But because it
shouldn't be the dictum

00:28:40.550 --> 00:28:48.620 line:84%
to have cast and crew that
is reflective of the world

00:28:48.620 --> 00:28:50.270 line:90%
that we live in.

00:28:50.270 --> 00:28:55.010 line:84%
A world that is not
centered in whiteness.

00:28:55.010 --> 00:29:01.490 line:84%
A world that is not
centered in one gender.

00:29:01.490 --> 00:29:05.180 line:84%
And it shouldn't
be the exception

00:29:05.180 --> 00:29:07.080 line:90%
that oh, wow, we've done this.

00:29:07.080 --> 00:29:11.120 line:84%
It should be the norm
that the all the beautiful

00:29:11.120 --> 00:29:14.000 line:84%
faces I'm seeing in
this conversation,

00:29:14.000 --> 00:29:16.340 line:84%
that I get to see
representatives

00:29:16.340 --> 00:29:19.685 line:84%
of our communities
working on our projects.

00:29:20.600 --> 00:29:26.130 line:84%
So for clarity, it's like we
shouldn't have to have it.

00:29:26.130 --> 00:29:29.240 line:84%
But until things
change, we should.

00:29:29.240 --> 00:29:34.790 line:84%
Because obviously the people
who are making decisions

00:29:34.790 --> 00:29:41.570 line:84%
and green lighting projects
are seeing things through,

00:29:41.570 --> 00:29:47.800 line:84%
historically, a
very narrow prism.

00:29:47.800 --> 00:29:53.170 line:84%
I mean in 1963, the NAACP
went to Hollywood and said,

00:29:53.170 --> 00:29:59.470 line:84%
would you at least please hire
one Negro on your production?

00:29:59.470 --> 00:30:02.020 line:90%
So yes, we have made progress.

00:30:02.020 --> 00:30:06.440 line:84%
But there is tremendous more
progress that needs to be made.

00:30:06.440 --> 00:30:12.670 line:84%
And if it needs to happen via
a writer to give awareness

00:30:12.670 --> 00:30:15.670 line:84%
to the incredible
talent that is out there

00:30:15.670 --> 00:30:20.050 line:84%
but hasn't had the opportunity,
well then, so be it.

00:30:20.050 --> 00:30:21.450 line:90%
Hear, hear.

00:30:21.450 --> 00:30:23.420 line:90%
Yeah.

00:30:23.420 --> 00:30:28.970 line:84%
Listening to Jim, I think
part of what you're describing

00:30:28.970 --> 00:30:31.490 line:90%
is what--

00:30:31.490 --> 00:30:35.420 line:84%
Roger and Lisa will be familiar
with this-- the black tax.

00:30:35.420 --> 00:30:40.250 line:84%
You want to be better, because
you don't want to mess it up

00:30:40.250 --> 00:30:41.840 line:90%
for everybody else

00:30:41.840 --> 00:30:46.730 line:84%
And I see nods, and I
see people feeling that.

00:30:46.730 --> 00:30:51.290 line:84%
And I think what is important
to recognize about that is most

00:30:51.290 --> 00:30:53.630 line:90%
of us willingly take that on.

00:30:53.630 --> 00:30:56.890 line:84%
I want people who
look like all of us

00:30:56.890 --> 00:31:00.230 line:84%
to keep-- because I know how
hard it is to do what we do.

00:31:00.935 --> 00:31:02.060 line:90%
I want to mentor everybody.

00:31:02.060 --> 00:31:03.590 line:84%
I want to answer
every phone call.

00:31:03.590 --> 00:31:05.360 line:90%
I want to do all those things.

00:31:05.360 --> 00:31:07.220 line:90%
But it is taxing.

00:31:07.220 --> 00:31:14.260 line:84%
And when our skill is
creativity and focus,

00:31:14.260 --> 00:31:17.150 line:90%
it really becomes a lot.

00:31:17.150 --> 00:31:21.390 line:84%
You find yourself spread too
thin out of a desire to help.

00:31:21.390 --> 00:31:25.040 line:84%
And so I do think
as much as we know--

00:31:25.040 --> 00:31:27.020 line:90%
I know Lisa helps everybody.

00:31:27.020 --> 00:31:30.710 line:84%
Like Lisa is
constantly answering

00:31:30.710 --> 00:31:32.240 line:84%
phone calls,
answering questions.

00:31:32.240 --> 00:31:33.210 line:90%
I know all--

00:31:33.210 --> 00:31:34.210 line:90%
I don't know about that.

00:31:34.210 --> 00:31:37.800 line:84%
But I'm thinking that
all of us do that.

00:31:37.800 --> 00:31:40.160 line:84%
And I think that
there's something

00:31:40.160 --> 00:31:42.860 line:84%
we should be thinking
about in doing that.

00:31:42.860 --> 00:31:46.520 line:84%
Like if we're spending
25% of our time doing

00:31:46.520 --> 00:31:51.530 line:84%
that, it's 25% it's less
time doing good work.

00:31:51.530 --> 00:31:53.300 line:90%
And it does take a toll.

00:31:53.300 --> 00:31:57.710 line:84%
So let's be honest
here, you can't really

00:31:57.710 --> 00:32:00.950 line:84%
be as selective in your help
as you would like to be,

00:32:00.950 --> 00:32:03.710 line:84%
because you're trying so
desperately to give help

00:32:03.710 --> 00:32:04.510 line:90%
where it is needed.

00:32:04.510 --> 00:32:07.300 line:84%
And you're so needed
in such a wide variety.

00:32:07.300 --> 00:32:09.500 line:84%
So that that's one thing
I'm just thinking about.

00:32:09.500 --> 00:32:12.770 line:84%
I'm wondering how you all deal
with it and what you think.

00:32:12.770 --> 00:32:16.910 line:84%
The other thing is I am very
excited about the Academy's

00:32:16.910 --> 00:32:19.120 line:90%
best picture requirements.

00:32:19.120 --> 00:32:22.920 line:84%
It's not until 2024, and
we will see what happens.

00:32:22.920 --> 00:32:25.340 line:84%
But I think we have
an opportunity now.

00:32:25.340 --> 00:32:29.660 line:84%
We have these next few years to
make that not a "check the box"

00:32:29.660 --> 00:32:35.870 line:84%
situation but to make it
a real, substantive, kind

00:32:35.870 --> 00:32:37.823 line:90%
of field building.

00:32:37.823 --> 00:32:39.240 line:84%
Because that's
what I want to see.

00:32:39.240 --> 00:32:43.460 line:84%
I want to see the field
seeded and built so that--

00:32:43.460 --> 00:32:45.980 line:84%
there's so many people
of color and people

00:32:45.980 --> 00:32:48.380 line:84%
with disabilities and people
from geographic backgrounds

00:32:48.380 --> 00:32:52.190 line:84%
that you're just picking the
right person for the project

00:32:52.190 --> 00:32:53.810 line:90%
instead of--

00:32:53.810 --> 00:32:56.550 line:84%
and I think we all want
to be known for our work.

00:32:56.550 --> 00:32:58.910 line:84%
I don't want to be picked
because I'm a Black woman, as

00:32:58.910 --> 00:33:00.290 line:90%
proud as I am of that.

00:33:00.290 --> 00:33:02.180 line:84%
I want to be picked
because I'm suited

00:33:02.180 --> 00:33:05.120 line:84%
to the material for
any number of reasons--

00:33:05.120 --> 00:33:08.530 line:90%
for my style, for my whatever.

00:33:08.530 --> 00:33:12.740 line:84%
So I guess I just throw
that out to all of us.

00:33:12.740 --> 00:33:15.890 line:84%
Well, Dawn, let's talk
a little bit about that.

00:33:15.890 --> 00:33:17.510 line:90%
Because--

00:33:17.510 --> 00:33:22.310 line:84%
Can I-- sorry-- touch upon this
question before we move on?

00:33:22.310 --> 00:33:24.710 line:84%
It will continue on to
what Dawn was saying.

00:33:24.710 --> 00:33:28.460 line:84%
I think what-- when we asked
a question, who's the best

00:33:28.460 --> 00:33:30.170 line:90%
person to tell the story?

00:33:30.170 --> 00:33:33.890 line:84%
And we do-- these projects
get picked in terms of someone

00:33:33.890 --> 00:33:35.630 line:90%
from that community.

00:33:35.630 --> 00:33:37.040 line:84%
Immediately, a
lot of people just

00:33:37.040 --> 00:33:40.130 line:84%
want to discredit and
discount that experience

00:33:40.130 --> 00:33:42.980 line:84%
as being valuable
and as some sort

00:33:42.980 --> 00:33:46.580 line:84%
of-- as a skill set to bring
to that project in many ways.

00:33:46.580 --> 00:33:51.890 line:84%
And what Jim was saying, there
was a great burden for me

00:33:51.890 --> 00:33:55.400 line:84%
to tell the story of Be Water,
because it was Bruce Lee.

00:33:55.400 --> 00:33:59.810 line:84%
And as an Asian-American
male having the privilege

00:33:59.810 --> 00:34:02.780 line:84%
to tell the story of Bruce
Lee, I saw it as a privilege.

00:34:02.780 --> 00:34:05.270 line:84%
Because we don't
have so many heroes,

00:34:05.270 --> 00:34:06.980 line:84%
so many stories, so
many myths that we

00:34:06.980 --> 00:34:09.920 line:84%
can take for granted
like other people do.

00:34:09.920 --> 00:34:15.580 line:84%
We have to, sadly, succeed
and knock it out of the park

00:34:15.580 --> 00:34:16.330 line:90%
a lot of the time.

00:34:16.330 --> 00:34:19.100 line:84%
Because we don't have these
opportunities so much.

00:34:19.100 --> 00:34:22.010 line:84%
And I think what Dawn
was saying too about

00:34:22.010 --> 00:34:25.310 line:84%
like having the responsibility
to kind of pay it forward,

00:34:25.310 --> 00:34:28.429 line:84%
but not always having the
time for ourselves to do that.

00:34:28.429 --> 00:34:32.060 line:84%
And I think in many
ways, us doing our work,

00:34:32.060 --> 00:34:34.219 line:84%
being authentic, is a
way to pay it forward.

00:34:34.219 --> 00:34:36.260 line:84%
Because we are we're
helping build lanes,

00:34:36.260 --> 00:34:38.420 line:84%
helping build space for
the future for people

00:34:38.420 --> 00:34:40.909 line:90%
to see that oh, if--

00:34:40.909 --> 00:34:43.670 line:84%
Bao was able to make a film
about an Asian-American

00:34:43.670 --> 00:34:47.480 line:84%
and make it be about
his Asian-Americaness.

00:34:47.480 --> 00:34:49.909 line:90%
That just creates more ideas.

00:34:49.909 --> 00:34:54.000 line:84%
And we're working in a space of
imagination sometimes in film.

00:34:54.000 --> 00:34:56.300 line:84%
We want to build more
spaces for imagination

00:34:56.300 --> 00:35:00.590 line:84%
and for us, documentary
filmmakers, imagined realities.

00:35:00.590 --> 00:35:05.150 line:84%
And I think when we're talking
to other filmmakers who

00:35:05.150 --> 00:35:08.900 line:84%
might be white male that
have more experience,

00:35:08.900 --> 00:35:11.000 line:84%
they're not judged
in the same way.

00:35:11.000 --> 00:35:14.350 line:84%
Because they don't have that
same type of background.

00:35:14.350 --> 00:35:17.210 line:84%
And again, we don't
value representation.

00:35:17.210 --> 00:35:19.570 line:84%
And we don't value experience
and lived experience,

00:35:19.570 --> 00:35:21.580 line:84%
and how that
contributes to a project

00:35:21.580 --> 00:35:26.290 line:84%
like how our pains our
struggles relate to that story.

00:35:26.290 --> 00:35:28.460 line:90%
And I think that's so important.

00:35:28.460 --> 00:35:31.150 line:84%
And when we think about
quote unquote "diversity"

00:35:31.150 --> 00:35:34.180 line:84%
and "representation,"
I feel like I

00:35:34.180 --> 00:35:36.580 line:84%
can tell Bruce Lee's story
in an Asian-American way,

00:35:36.580 --> 00:35:39.010 line:90%
because I've been harassed.

00:35:39.010 --> 00:35:42.040 line:84%
I've been discriminated
as an Asian-American.

00:35:42.040 --> 00:35:44.230 line:84%
And although other
filmmakers who

00:35:44.230 --> 00:35:46.480 line:84%
are not part of my
community can understand it,

00:35:46.480 --> 00:35:47.440 line:90%
can they feel it?

00:35:47.440 --> 00:35:50.000 line:90%
And I think that's important.

00:35:50.000 --> 00:36:00.030 line:84%
Dawn, we know that in the
industry, whenever there's

00:36:00.030 --> 00:36:02.990 line:84%
a Black project
with Black content,

00:36:02.990 --> 00:36:05.720 line:90%
there is a list of five--

00:36:05.720 --> 00:36:07.270 line:90%
you, me, a few other people.

00:36:07.270 --> 00:36:09.855 line:84%
--I feel like I should
just call you, and be like,

00:36:09.855 --> 00:36:13.040 line:90%
do you want to do this?

00:36:13.040 --> 00:36:16.480 line:84%
But I just want to talk about
that, because it's so crazy.

00:36:16.480 --> 00:36:19.620 line:84%
Why did they go to
the same five people?

00:36:19.620 --> 00:36:21.145 line:90%
Do they think we don't know?

00:36:23.830 --> 00:36:25.240 line:90%
Like guess what?

00:36:25.240 --> 00:36:28.210 line:84%
Yes, there is a [? Black ?]
[? back ?] phone.

00:36:28.210 --> 00:36:30.280 line:84%
And we pick it up
after you call.

00:36:30.280 --> 00:36:32.850 line:90%
We're like, did they call you?

00:36:32.850 --> 00:36:33.980 line:90%
What do you think?

00:36:33.980 --> 00:36:34.930 line:90%
Oh, are you busy?

00:36:34.930 --> 00:36:36.610 line:90%
OK, who should we give this to?

00:36:36.610 --> 00:36:39.700 line:90%
I mean-- yes.

00:36:39.700 --> 00:36:41.440 line:90%
What was your question?

00:36:41.440 --> 00:36:45.850 line:84%
I just-- how do we
open up the field?

00:36:45.850 --> 00:36:49.380 line:84%
How do we-- what do
we do to change that?

00:36:49.380 --> 00:36:52.130 line:90%
I desperately need to change.

00:36:52.130 --> 00:36:54.550 line:84%
I mean, I've had this
conversation with so many

00:36:54.550 --> 00:36:58.240 line:90%
people and trying to say, hey--

00:36:58.240 --> 00:37:01.240 line:84%
I feel like-- I'm not going to
mention this filmmaker's name.

00:37:01.240 --> 00:37:04.180 line:84%
Because I don't know if he
is getting all the calls.

00:37:04.180 --> 00:37:09.610 line:84%
But I feel like I send
people so many times to like

00:37:09.610 --> 00:37:11.090 line:90%
a few people.

00:37:11.090 --> 00:37:13.240 line:84%
And I don't see him
getting those jobs.

00:37:13.240 --> 00:37:17.745 line:84%
And I'm like-- yeah,
it needs to change.

00:37:20.360 --> 00:37:25.920 line:84%
But also like I feel
like I can't change it.

00:37:25.920 --> 00:37:32.070 line:84%
And that is part of
a frustration is--

00:37:32.070 --> 00:37:34.500 line:90%
I'm worried about my own career.

00:37:34.500 --> 00:37:36.780 line:90%
I'm not a teacher anymore.

00:37:36.780 --> 00:37:42.360 line:84%
So you all-- by "you all," I
mean people with the ability

00:37:42.360 --> 00:37:45.660 line:84%
to say you, person, you
should do this movie--

00:37:45.660 --> 00:37:47.620 line:90%
do your own work.

00:37:47.620 --> 00:37:50.040 line:90%
And train your own folks.

00:37:50.040 --> 00:37:52.560 line:90%
And do it for real.

00:37:52.560 --> 00:37:55.410 line:90%
There are such talented people.

00:37:55.410 --> 00:37:56.550 line:90%
And that is why--

00:37:56.550 --> 00:37:58.050 line:84%
and we should talk
about this-- that

00:37:58.050 --> 00:38:01.740 line:84%
is why there is so much
anger and hostility when

00:38:01.740 --> 00:38:06.450 line:84%
a person without experience
gets to do a premium project.

00:38:06.450 --> 00:38:13.110 line:84%
And that is why individual
people, beloved or not aside--

00:38:13.110 --> 00:38:15.150 line:84%
that is why that
frustration happens.

00:38:15.150 --> 00:38:20.070 line:84%
Because we, as makers of
color, are told so long,

00:38:20.070 --> 00:38:22.620 line:84%
I can't take a chance on
that first time filmmaker,

00:38:22.620 --> 00:38:24.340 line:90%
because this is so important.

00:38:24.340 --> 00:38:27.870 line:84%
So when that rule is
not applied to people

00:38:27.870 --> 00:38:31.055 line:90%
who are of European descent--

00:38:31.055 --> 00:38:32.430 line:84%
and I say that
very specifically,

00:38:32.430 --> 00:38:33.840 line:90%
because I'm reading Caste.

00:38:33.840 --> 00:38:36.060 line:84%
And I now know the word
"Caucasian" is made up.

00:38:36.060 --> 00:38:42.120 line:84%
And white is a construct
of colonialism and castism.

00:38:42.120 --> 00:38:45.330 line:84%
So that is why that
is so enraging.

00:38:45.330 --> 00:38:49.180 line:84%
Because yeah, you
can when you want to.

00:38:49.180 --> 00:38:52.860 line:84%
And so what is it going to need
to take for you to want to?

00:38:52.860 --> 00:38:58.703 line:84%
I don't know if it's just like
turning down a bunch of things.

00:38:58.703 --> 00:39:00.870 line:84%
Maybe we should use the
[? Black ?] [? back ?] phone

00:39:00.870 --> 00:39:01.440 line:90%
for that.

00:39:04.050 --> 00:39:09.540 line:84%
Well, a lot of times it's
like you passed the name on,

00:39:09.540 --> 00:39:11.340 line:84%
but it's also
forcing those buyers

00:39:11.340 --> 00:39:14.220 line:84%
because you have power
now to force those buyers,

00:39:14.220 --> 00:39:15.673 line:90%
you work with this filmmaker.

00:39:15.673 --> 00:39:17.340 line:84%
You're going to work
with this filmmaker

00:39:17.340 --> 00:39:19.007 line:84%
and make them work
without [INAUDIBLE]..

00:39:19.007 --> 00:39:21.630 line:84%
I think that's what you're
doing with your company, right,

00:39:21.630 --> 00:39:25.410 line:84%
is saying, OK, I'm
going to be the EP,

00:39:25.410 --> 00:39:27.520 line:84%
but I'm going to
choose the filmmaker.

00:39:27.520 --> 00:39:30.510 line:84%
And so shifting the
dynamics so that you

00:39:30.510 --> 00:39:34.710 line:84%
have to actively dismiss
the person, and in doing so,

00:39:34.710 --> 00:39:38.250 line:84%
dismiss your judgment as
the leader of the project.

00:39:38.250 --> 00:39:43.950 line:84%
So I think that is hopefully
something that we can move to.

00:39:43.950 --> 00:39:47.100 line:90%
But I feel like--

00:39:47.100 --> 00:39:51.300 line:84%
I feel like we need to get a
lot of these buyers in the room

00:39:51.300 --> 00:39:52.740 line:90%
and say--

00:39:52.740 --> 00:39:53.880 line:90%
I'll just speak for myself.

00:39:53.880 --> 00:39:56.153 line:84%
I do not-- I have no
joy of being the only--

00:39:56.153 --> 00:39:58.320 line:84%
not that I'm the only-- but
do you know what I mean?

00:39:58.320 --> 00:40:01.080 line:84%
I have no joy in that,
because that means--

00:40:04.140 --> 00:40:08.080 line:90%
that is not what it should be--

00:40:08.080 --> 00:40:08.580 line:90%
anyway.

00:40:11.070 --> 00:40:13.310 line:84%
Can I talk about my
experience for a second?

00:40:13.310 --> 00:40:14.040 line:90%
Yeah.

00:40:14.040 --> 00:40:15.910 line:90%
I certainly even as a--

00:40:15.910 --> 00:40:17.580 line:90%
I was a sound mixer--

00:40:17.580 --> 00:40:20.640 line:90%
still am-- for many, many years.

00:40:20.640 --> 00:40:23.640 line:84%
But as a personal
disability, I always

00:40:23.640 --> 00:40:25.680 line:84%
came into things
thinking, I've got

00:40:25.680 --> 00:40:28.350 line:84%
to prove that I'm
not incompetent.

00:40:28.350 --> 00:40:33.720 line:84%
Like I live under this fear
that people will look at me

00:40:33.720 --> 00:40:36.600 line:84%
and say gee, I don't know
if that guy can do the job,

00:40:36.600 --> 00:40:40.260 line:84%
simply because I'm
sitting in a wheelchair.

00:40:40.260 --> 00:40:43.330 line:84%
And the experience
with Crip Camp--

00:40:43.330 --> 00:40:46.740 line:84%
Crip Camp was made by
myself and Nicole Newnham--

00:40:46.740 --> 00:40:51.100 line:84%
very experienced, wonderful
documentary filmmaker.

00:40:51.100 --> 00:40:54.500 line:90%
And our collaboration that--

00:40:54.500 --> 00:40:59.120 line:84%
part of the collaboration that
I thought was pretty amazing

00:40:59.120 --> 00:41:03.020 line:84%
was that here was this
able-bodied filmmaker working

00:41:03.020 --> 00:41:06.420 line:84%
with someone who
uses a wheelchair.

00:41:06.420 --> 00:41:11.290 line:84%
And she could see all the
things I wasn't able to access--

00:41:11.290 --> 00:41:14.200 line:84%
simple things like
networking parties

00:41:14.200 --> 00:41:17.830 line:84%
that are way up a flight
of stairs and such.

00:41:17.830 --> 00:41:20.490 line:90%
And it was really our allyship.

00:41:20.490 --> 00:41:23.170 line:90%
But her allyship to me--

00:41:23.170 --> 00:41:28.570 line:84%
that really helped enabled
me as a first time director

00:41:28.570 --> 00:41:32.740 line:84%
to really be a director
and producer with her

00:41:32.740 --> 00:41:33.415 line:90%
on this project.

00:41:36.750 --> 00:41:40.050 line:84%
So I just think that is a
kind of an important thing

00:41:40.050 --> 00:41:42.850 line:90%
to think about.

00:41:42.850 --> 00:41:47.430 line:84%
I wouldn't be-- I wouldn't
be here if it wasn't for her

00:41:47.430 --> 00:41:50.880 line:84%
and how we were able to
craft a collaboration.

00:41:50.880 --> 00:41:53.070 line:84%
She wouldn't be here
if it wasn't for you.

00:41:53.070 --> 00:41:57.030 line:84%
And it totally breaks my
heart to hear you say--

00:41:57.030 --> 00:42:02.040 line:84%
because I think many people
have had those same feelings of,

00:42:02.040 --> 00:42:08.250 line:84%
I'm a fraud, or I'm going to be
thought of as not good enough.

00:42:08.250 --> 00:42:11.810 line:84%
And your reputation is
so stellar that just--

00:42:11.810 --> 00:42:13.150 line:90%
I find that surprising.

00:42:13.150 --> 00:42:18.150 line:84%
So I really value
you saying that.

00:42:18.150 --> 00:42:21.690 line:84%
I remember when Jason
did When I Walk.

00:42:21.690 --> 00:42:23.490 line:90%
Jason DaSilva, yeah.

00:42:23.490 --> 00:42:29.910 line:84%
And there was the New
York Sundance party

00:42:29.910 --> 00:42:32.430 line:90%
for directors in New York City.

00:42:32.430 --> 00:42:36.780 line:84%
And he gets there and it was
up a very narrow, creaky flight

00:42:36.780 --> 00:42:37.620 line:90%
of stairs.

00:42:37.620 --> 00:42:41.400 line:84%
So what was meant to be a
celebration of all of us

00:42:41.400 --> 00:42:45.270 line:84%
who were thrilled to be accepted
by the Sundance Film Festival.

00:42:45.270 --> 00:42:48.990 line:84%
And know somebody
eventually carried him,

00:42:48.990 --> 00:42:52.410 line:84%
but that's not how he
wanted to enter the room.

00:42:52.410 --> 00:42:55.110 line:84%
There was a loss of
control, a little bit

00:42:55.110 --> 00:43:00.660 line:84%
of loss of dignity for him
to have to ask and then

00:43:00.660 --> 00:43:03.300 line:84%
have all of these accommodations
that should have been,

00:43:03.300 --> 00:43:06.360 line:84%
and easily could have
been, worked out before.

00:43:06.360 --> 00:43:09.600 line:84%
Either find another
place or make sure

00:43:09.600 --> 00:43:12.870 line:84%
that he is situated
early enough,

00:43:12.870 --> 00:43:16.960 line:84%
so he doesn't have to
come into the room.

00:43:16.960 --> 00:43:18.520 line:90%
And I could see it.

00:43:18.520 --> 00:43:19.330 line:90%
He was angry.

00:43:19.330 --> 00:43:21.480 line:90%
He was upset.

00:43:21.480 --> 00:43:25.530 line:84%
He couldn't walk-- come into
the room triumphantly the way

00:43:25.530 --> 00:43:27.840 line:84%
that he should have to
celebrate his accomplishment.

00:43:27.840 --> 00:43:31.590 line:90%
So that is--

00:43:31.590 --> 00:43:33.090 line:84%
I think it's
important for people

00:43:33.090 --> 00:43:39.480 line:84%
to hear those experiences
so that hopefully--

00:43:39.480 --> 00:43:41.070 line:90%
and I know it's a burden.

00:43:41.070 --> 00:43:43.380 line:84%
Because it's a burden
for me and for all of you

00:43:43.380 --> 00:43:49.422 line:84%
to constantly inject race
and things sometimes.

00:43:49.422 --> 00:43:51.380 line:84%
You don't want to just
talk about those things.

00:43:51.380 --> 00:43:52.797 line:84%
You want to talk
about everything.

00:43:52.797 --> 00:43:58.380 line:84%
But I do really value you
continually reminding us.

00:43:58.380 --> 00:44:01.650 line:84%
Because a lot of,
I think, behavior

00:44:01.650 --> 00:44:05.740 line:84%
that is offensive or off
putting is not intentional.

00:44:05.740 --> 00:44:10.650 line:84%
And so I think people need to be
reminded of their own blindness

00:44:10.650 --> 00:44:12.750 line:84%
like Roger was
saying with Prudence.

00:44:12.750 --> 00:44:15.780 line:84%
You need-- there was just
a learning curve there.

00:44:15.780 --> 00:44:18.720 line:84%
So like you having us
all describe ourselves--

00:44:18.720 --> 00:44:20.850 line:90%
I love doing it as you can see--

00:44:20.850 --> 00:44:23.280 line:90%
a little bit of the actor in me.

00:44:23.280 --> 00:44:26.010 line:84%
And just the fact
of you asking for us

00:44:26.010 --> 00:44:28.500 line:84%
to do that very simple
thing, hopefully, that

00:44:28.500 --> 00:44:32.160 line:84%
will encourage other people to
think of that when you're not

00:44:32.160 --> 00:44:36.030 line:90%
in the room or on the Zoom.

00:44:36.030 --> 00:44:38.430 line:84%
I think there's a part
of this in which we

00:44:38.430 --> 00:44:43.330 line:84%
are filmmakers and artists,
but we're also activists.

00:44:43.330 --> 00:44:50.170 line:84%
And that it would be
nice to be able to maybe

00:44:50.170 --> 00:44:53.050 line:84%
be more of an
artist certain days

00:44:53.050 --> 00:44:58.270 line:84%
than an activist for my
community and for other people.

00:44:58.270 --> 00:45:01.990 line:84%
Let's talk a little
bit about allies.

00:45:01.990 --> 00:45:05.530 line:84%
So there is-- where does
the responsibility lie?

00:45:08.940 --> 00:45:12.680 line:84%
There's been some calling
out of white filmmakers

00:45:12.680 --> 00:45:19.210 line:84%
who've helicoptered in and
told stories about people

00:45:19.210 --> 00:45:21.850 line:84%
from underrepresented
communities.

00:45:21.850 --> 00:45:27.190 line:84%
The work around that has been
done by the Brown Girls Doc

00:45:27.190 --> 00:45:30.490 line:84%
Mafia and [? ADOC. ?]
And all of these are--

00:45:30.490 --> 00:45:33.310 line:84%
is it our responsibility
to educate?

00:45:33.310 --> 00:45:37.150 line:84%
Is it our responsibility
to actually help

00:45:37.150 --> 00:45:39.470 line:90%
people understand the issues?

00:45:39.470 --> 00:45:42.670 line:84%
Or does some of
that responsibility

00:45:42.670 --> 00:45:45.670 line:90%
lie in the white community?

00:45:45.670 --> 00:45:49.030 line:84%
I'd love to, Lisa,
talk about like that.

00:45:49.030 --> 00:45:51.340 line:84%
Because it seems
like all the work

00:45:51.340 --> 00:45:54.190 line:84%
is being done by
these organizations.

00:45:54.190 --> 00:45:57.293 line:84%
And I don't see that much
coming from the people

00:45:57.293 --> 00:45:58.960 line:84%
who actually hold the
power-- people who

00:45:58.960 --> 00:46:00.440 line:90%
have the white privilege.

00:46:00.440 --> 00:46:03.010 line:84%
I don't see that
coming from them.

00:46:03.010 --> 00:46:06.250 line:84%
I see the work coming
from people of--

00:46:06.250 --> 00:46:09.080 line:84%
people from underrepresented
communities.

00:46:09.080 --> 00:46:15.940 line:84%
And then what you see is proof
positive of the difficulties

00:46:15.940 --> 00:46:18.380 line:90%
that we have.

00:46:18.380 --> 00:46:21.310 line:84%
I'm kind of tired
of doing the work.

00:46:21.310 --> 00:46:24.790 line:90%
I'm tired of being 300%.

00:46:24.790 --> 00:46:28.960 line:84%
And I think probably all of
us and so many of the people

00:46:28.960 --> 00:46:33.280 line:84%
that we prize in our
community feel the same way.

00:46:33.280 --> 00:46:35.500 line:84%
When someone says
they're an ally,

00:46:35.500 --> 00:46:38.200 line:90%
I don't need you to be my ally.

00:46:38.200 --> 00:46:39.970 line:90%
Because that means that you're--

00:46:39.970 --> 00:46:43.210 line:84%
almost infers you're
doing me a favor.

00:46:43.210 --> 00:46:48.340 line:84%
I want you to be someone
who is in lockstep with me

00:46:48.340 --> 00:46:54.520 line:84%
for change, for access, for
opportunity, for extending

00:46:54.520 --> 00:46:58.990 line:84%
beyond the five
Black directors who--

00:46:58.990 --> 00:47:03.070 line:90%
or any kind of directors--

00:47:03.070 --> 00:47:07.330 line:84%
and that you are
seeing your privilege.

00:47:07.330 --> 00:47:10.220 line:84%
You're sitting with
your microaggression.

00:47:10.220 --> 00:47:18.380 line:84%
You are making the
change as a gatekeeper

00:47:18.380 --> 00:47:23.660 line:84%
and realizing that you
have fallen off the wagon.

00:47:23.660 --> 00:47:27.200 line:90%
You haven't done your homework.

00:47:27.200 --> 00:47:29.180 line:84%
You know there is
certainly something

00:47:29.180 --> 00:47:33.530 line:84%
to be said about being
in a pod for COVID.

00:47:33.530 --> 00:47:38.120 line:84%
But I think then we have to
get out of our creative pods.

00:47:40.880 --> 00:47:46.700 line:84%
For those who have the
ability to award projects--

00:47:46.700 --> 00:47:53.510 line:84%
premium projects-- and
to extend themselves

00:47:53.510 --> 00:47:58.310 line:90%
to new people in our community.

00:48:01.230 --> 00:48:02.900 line:90%
Look at Dawn's film--

00:48:02.900 --> 00:48:06.650 line:84%
look at our films
and see who we hire,

00:48:06.650 --> 00:48:09.290 line:90%
if you don't know who to hire.

00:48:09.290 --> 00:48:10.850 line:90%
That's what I would say.

00:48:10.850 --> 00:48:12.950 line:90%
Let's just start there.

00:48:12.950 --> 00:48:19.340 line:84%
We've planted so many seeds
or so many breadcrumbs,

00:48:19.340 --> 00:48:25.520 line:84%
but there is a great amount
of frustration about it

00:48:25.520 --> 00:48:33.340 line:84%
than transfiguring into
actual work and change.

00:48:33.340 --> 00:48:37.310 line:84%
Do people in power
ever see their power?

00:48:37.310 --> 00:48:38.970 line:90%
I mean, is there--

00:48:38.970 --> 00:48:40.190 line:90%
they're just going to--

00:48:40.190 --> 00:48:45.440 line:84%
about these gatekeepers,
the people in power

00:48:45.440 --> 00:48:48.350 line:84%
who make the decisions,
are they really going

00:48:48.350 --> 00:48:51.560 line:90%
to just give up their power?

00:48:51.560 --> 00:48:53.185 line:84%
That's not a question
for me to answer.

00:48:53.185 --> 00:48:56.570 line:84%
[INAUDIBLE] But I
think Lisa brings up

00:48:56.570 --> 00:48:59.420 line:84%
a good point of the difference
really between like allyship

00:48:59.420 --> 00:49:00.930 line:90%
versus solidarity.

00:49:00.930 --> 00:49:03.740 line:84%
I want you to stand
in solidarity with us.

00:49:03.740 --> 00:49:05.390 line:84%
We're all part of
the same community.

00:49:05.390 --> 00:49:07.460 line:84%
You're not doing
something for me

00:49:07.460 --> 00:49:11.265 line:84%
because you're
higher up in a way.

00:49:11.265 --> 00:49:12.890 line:84%
My parents are
Vietnamese war refugees,

00:49:12.890 --> 00:49:18.440 line:84%
so my history of cinema
and my self reflection

00:49:18.440 --> 00:49:20.780 line:90%
is through the Vietnam War.

00:49:20.780 --> 00:49:23.210 line:84%
And that's always the
way I censored myself

00:49:23.210 --> 00:49:25.100 line:84%
in many ways when I
saw myself on screen.

00:49:25.100 --> 00:49:26.960 line:90%
I was a body count.

00:49:26.960 --> 00:49:27.680 line:90%
I was a peasant.

00:49:27.680 --> 00:49:30.380 line:90%
I was just someone who--

00:49:30.380 --> 00:49:31.910 line:90%
shoot off a helicopter.

00:49:31.910 --> 00:49:33.770 line:90%
I never saw myself exist.

00:49:33.770 --> 00:49:37.340 line:84%
And I think people
need to realize

00:49:37.340 --> 00:49:40.190 line:84%
how powerful these
depictions are, especially

00:49:40.190 --> 00:49:40.940 line:90%
for young people.

00:49:40.940 --> 00:49:43.610 line:84%
When they see themselves, when
they see they don't exist,

00:49:43.610 --> 00:49:47.330 line:84%
they see that they have nothing
to strive for or exist in.

00:49:47.330 --> 00:49:48.950 line:90%
I think we have to--

00:49:48.950 --> 00:49:52.480 line:84%
before we can move forward, we
have to like reckon the past.

00:49:52.480 --> 00:49:56.580 line:84%
We have to re-examine
all the dirty truth

00:49:56.580 --> 00:50:00.440 line:84%
of cinematic history,
of America's history.

00:50:00.440 --> 00:50:05.690 line:84%
And before we figure out how
these gatekeepers can "help"

00:50:05.690 --> 00:50:08.593 line:84%
us, quote unquote, they
have to look at their own--

00:50:08.593 --> 00:50:10.760 line:84%
they need to learn from
their own past instead of us

00:50:10.760 --> 00:50:14.720 line:84%
teaching them what they
did to us in many ways.

00:50:14.720 --> 00:50:15.220 line:90%
Yeah.

00:50:15.220 --> 00:50:16.790 line:90%
Yeah.

00:50:16.790 --> 00:50:20.970 line:84%
I wanted to talk briefly
about each of your films.

00:50:20.970 --> 00:50:31.490 line:84%
Dawn, Good Trouble-- obviously
such a towering figure.

00:50:31.490 --> 00:50:33.590 line:84%
What do you feel that
you brought to that film

00:50:33.590 --> 00:50:37.390 line:90%
as a Black woman?

00:50:37.390 --> 00:50:38.920 line:90%
I think a lot.

00:50:38.920 --> 00:50:40.900 line:84%
I'm really like--
I'm writing down

00:50:40.900 --> 00:50:42.850 line:84%
everything you're saying
about, because I'm

00:50:42.850 --> 00:50:45.220 line:84%
going to steal it and
appropriate it and take it

00:50:45.220 --> 00:50:48.220 line:90%
as my own.

00:50:48.220 --> 00:50:50.810 line:90%
But I think-- a few things.

00:50:50.810 --> 00:50:54.730 line:84%
One is Mr. Lewis is always
described as being brave.

00:50:54.730 --> 00:50:57.400 line:84%
And when we talk about
deconstructing racism,

00:50:57.400 --> 00:51:00.740 line:84%
I think one of the first
things I looked to is language.

00:51:00.740 --> 00:51:05.140 line:84%
So what John Lewis and the
other civil rights workers did

00:51:05.140 --> 00:51:06.750 line:90%
is strategy.

00:51:06.750 --> 00:51:11.200 line:84%
It was all strategy-- how they
dressed, where they protested,

00:51:11.200 --> 00:51:14.290 line:84%
what things they targeted,
what their demands were,

00:51:14.290 --> 00:51:15.860 line:84%
how they were all
going to behave.

00:51:15.860 --> 00:51:17.320 line:84%
They coordinated
how they were all

00:51:17.320 --> 00:51:19.630 line:84%
going to behave when
they were arrested.

00:51:19.630 --> 00:51:23.350 line:84%
So focusing on their
intelligence rather than

00:51:23.350 --> 00:51:28.990 line:84%
just their bodies being
put in front of a baton,

00:51:28.990 --> 00:51:31.060 line:90%
that was one big goal.

00:51:31.060 --> 00:51:32.320 line:90%
But I think also--

00:51:32.320 --> 00:51:38.650 line:84%
there was my great grandfather
was a pastor in a Black church.

00:51:38.650 --> 00:51:42.460 line:84%
And so I think that there
can be a misunderstanding

00:51:42.460 --> 00:51:45.710 line:84%
about the role of religion
in the Black community.

00:51:45.710 --> 00:51:51.290 line:84%
It's not just God
and spirituality.

00:51:51.290 --> 00:51:52.460 line:90%
It's real community.

00:51:52.460 --> 00:51:53.690 line:90%
It's a home.

00:51:53.690 --> 00:51:55.150 line:90%
It's a place of safety.

00:51:55.150 --> 00:51:58.460 line:84%
It's where you go for meals
and political meetings.

00:51:58.460 --> 00:52:02.660 line:84%
And so understanding the meaning
of the Black church, I think,

00:52:02.660 --> 00:52:05.540 line:84%
was really crucial
to understanding

00:52:05.540 --> 00:52:08.300 line:84%
why John Lewis and the
other civil rights workers

00:52:08.300 --> 00:52:10.280 line:90%
met in the basement of a church.

00:52:10.280 --> 00:52:12.710 line:84%
A number of the
people were ministers

00:52:12.710 --> 00:52:14.000 line:90%
who were leading them.

00:52:14.000 --> 00:52:17.090 line:84%
There's a tie in-- so
understanding that.

00:52:17.090 --> 00:52:18.710 line:84%
And then the last
thing I would say

00:52:18.710 --> 00:52:22.520 line:84%
is just the level of respect
that you'll give to elders.

00:52:22.520 --> 00:52:27.260 line:84%
Like nobody had to tell me
you don't call representative

00:52:27.260 --> 00:52:28.970 line:90%
Lewis, John.

00:52:28.970 --> 00:52:32.210 line:84%
Like my grandmother would
rise up and strangle me

00:52:32.210 --> 00:52:35.240 line:84%
if I referred to an
elder by a first name.

00:52:35.240 --> 00:52:37.670 line:84%
Even if he said to do
it which he never would.

00:52:37.670 --> 00:52:43.100 line:84%
But just I always came
to him with the respect

00:52:43.100 --> 00:52:44.450 line:90%
that you give to an elder.

00:52:44.450 --> 00:52:49.440 line:84%
And that was taught to me
from the time I could speak.

00:52:49.440 --> 00:52:55.070 line:84%
And I think he knew I understood
our relationship, which

00:52:55.070 --> 00:52:59.480 line:84%
is as much as he was kind
and open and generous,

00:52:59.480 --> 00:53:01.830 line:84%
he was always going
to be my elder.

00:53:01.830 --> 00:53:04.930 line:84%
And I was happy
for him to be that.

00:53:04.930 --> 00:53:09.230 line:84%
And I think that
helped him relax.

00:53:09.230 --> 00:53:13.190 line:84%
That helped him
to want to teach.

00:53:13.190 --> 00:53:16.760 line:84%
And I think a lot of the
interviews-- it's passing on.

00:53:16.760 --> 00:53:20.210 line:84%
It's that oral tradition
of passing things on.

00:53:20.210 --> 00:53:22.040 line:84%
So I think all of
that and that I

00:53:22.040 --> 00:53:25.130 line:84%
think is what part
of what I was saying

00:53:25.130 --> 00:53:30.050 line:84%
is all of that I
didn't have to learn.

00:53:30.050 --> 00:53:33.060 line:84%
I came to the project
with that knowledge.

00:53:33.060 --> 00:53:37.610 line:84%
And so I think I was the
person to tell that story.

00:53:37.610 --> 00:53:39.480 line:84%
But I did also ask
myself the question,

00:53:39.480 --> 00:53:42.620 line:90%
am I the person to do this?

00:53:42.620 --> 00:53:46.160 line:84%
Many people can do many
things about John Lewis,

00:53:46.160 --> 00:53:47.600 line:90%
and I hope that they will.

00:53:47.600 --> 00:53:49.400 line:84%
But I think that
those are the things,

00:53:49.400 --> 00:53:52.520 line:84%
those are the skills,
and also the memories,

00:53:52.520 --> 00:53:54.410 line:84%
and the experiences
that I wanted to lean

00:53:54.410 --> 00:53:56.230 line:90%
into in telling the story.

00:53:56.230 --> 00:53:57.140 line:90%
No, I hear you.

00:53:57.140 --> 00:54:05.090 line:84%
And Jim, let's talk a little bit
about your perspective making

00:54:05.090 --> 00:54:07.400 line:90%
Crip Camp.

00:54:07.400 --> 00:54:13.070 line:84%
For me, I realized after sort of
six months of being in Zimbabwe

00:54:13.070 --> 00:54:17.000 line:84%
with children with very
severe disabilities

00:54:17.000 --> 00:54:20.490 line:84%
that when I was doing
wheelchair races with them,

00:54:20.490 --> 00:54:25.130 line:84%
and they were like fake
faith healing each other.

00:54:25.130 --> 00:54:26.660 line:90%
And I was felt part of that.

00:54:26.660 --> 00:54:30.830 line:84%
And I realized that they were
just like any other kids.

00:54:30.830 --> 00:54:32.250 line:90%
That they were just like--

00:54:32.250 --> 00:54:36.440 line:84%
they just wanted to have fun and
have sex and talk about girls

00:54:36.440 --> 00:54:37.520 line:90%
and do--

00:54:37.520 --> 00:54:43.130 line:90%
and all that broke away.

00:54:43.130 --> 00:54:48.140 line:84%
Talk about Crip Camp and making
that film from your perspective

00:54:48.140 --> 00:54:52.420 line:90%
was revelatory for me.

00:54:52.420 --> 00:54:54.240 line:84%
Well, I mean, I
think that it was

00:54:54.240 --> 00:54:57.270 line:90%
revealing to a lot of people.

00:54:57.270 --> 00:54:59.850 line:84%
We're not looked
upon as people--

00:54:59.850 --> 00:55:03.450 line:84%
rarely we're looked
upon as sexual beings.

00:55:03.450 --> 00:55:06.010 line:90%
But we had this incredible--

00:55:06.010 --> 00:55:08.820 line:84%
Nicole and I had this
incredible 5 and 1/2

00:55:08.820 --> 00:55:12.210 line:84%
hours of this black and
white video footage from 1971

00:55:12.210 --> 00:55:13.900 line:90%
at this camp.

00:55:13.900 --> 00:55:16.780 line:90%
And there's joy there.

00:55:16.780 --> 00:55:20.020 line:90%
And there's humor.

00:55:20.020 --> 00:55:22.690 line:84%
And there's really kind
of deep consideration

00:55:22.690 --> 00:55:29.300 line:84%
that you just really rarely
see this intimate and inside

00:55:29.300 --> 00:55:31.310 line:90%
of a view.

00:55:31.310 --> 00:55:34.340 line:84%
I think that one of the things
that makes our film really

00:55:34.340 --> 00:55:35.900 line:84%
wonderful is that
this is really told

00:55:35.900 --> 00:55:38.090 line:84%
from the inside as
opposed to somebody

00:55:38.090 --> 00:55:40.400 line:90%
really looking down upon us.

00:55:40.400 --> 00:55:45.050 line:84%
And I appreciate that the
people's video theater, that

00:55:45.050 --> 00:55:48.950 line:84%
was there 50 years ago, didn't
go around the camp going, well,

00:55:48.950 --> 00:55:51.620 line:84%
camp director, tell us about
the handicapped people.

00:55:51.620 --> 00:55:56.470 line:84%
But they were right with us,
and they were giving us agency.

00:55:56.470 --> 00:56:00.820 line:84%
And I originally
asked Nicole if she

00:56:00.820 --> 00:56:03.280 line:84%
would direct a film
about the summer camp,

00:56:03.280 --> 00:56:04.630 line:90%
and she said we should--

00:56:04.630 --> 00:56:07.590 line:84%
she wanted me to
direct with her.

00:56:07.590 --> 00:56:12.570 line:84%
And that was-- it
definitely opened up a lot.

00:56:12.570 --> 00:56:16.860 line:84%
Because when I was
asking the questions

00:56:16.860 --> 00:56:21.760 line:84%
to someone within my
community, they trusted me.

00:56:21.760 --> 00:56:25.110 line:84%
They trust me that I would
take what they were telling,

00:56:25.110 --> 00:56:30.930 line:84%
and I would do right
by them and their life.

00:56:30.930 --> 00:56:33.120 line:90%
It was a big responsibility.

00:56:33.120 --> 00:56:33.900 line:90%
Absolutely.

00:56:33.900 --> 00:56:35.670 line:90%
Absolutely.

00:56:35.670 --> 00:56:40.120 line:84%
Lisa, you co-directed
with Liz Garbus.

00:56:40.120 --> 00:56:42.840 line:84%
What are the different
perspectives each of you--

00:56:42.840 --> 00:56:46.800 line:90%
you as a Black Latina woman--

00:56:46.800 --> 00:56:48.560 line:90%
Black Latin woman.

00:56:48.560 --> 00:56:51.390 line:84%
Liz-- what did you
talk about that?

00:56:54.030 --> 00:56:58.500 line:84%
It was a really
interesting collaboration.

00:56:58.500 --> 00:57:04.260 line:84%
Ultimately, we were both
passionate and committed

00:57:04.260 --> 00:57:11.610 line:84%
in interrogating this
history of voter suppression

00:57:11.610 --> 00:57:18.890 line:84%
in our country, which
is bedded in racism.

00:57:18.890 --> 00:57:21.770 line:90%
It's embedded in power.

00:57:21.770 --> 00:57:29.240 line:84%
And we had a hive mind
about the structure

00:57:29.240 --> 00:57:34.910 line:84%
and the approach, our
interviews, et cetera.

00:57:34.910 --> 00:57:41.750 line:84%
But we also had a space for
very healthy conversations

00:57:41.750 --> 00:57:47.090 line:84%
that allowed us to speak
from our unique vantage point

00:57:47.090 --> 00:57:48.860 line:90%
and experiences.

00:57:48.860 --> 00:57:52.880 line:84%
And it's in our
personal histories

00:57:52.880 --> 00:57:55.730 line:84%
and how it intersected with
the story that we were telling.

00:57:59.450 --> 00:58:02.870 line:84%
So honor, history,
and interrogation,

00:58:02.870 --> 00:58:10.640 line:84%
and discovery in documentary
films and as does Liz.

00:58:10.640 --> 00:58:16.940 line:84%
And I feel that it helped us to
make a story that is not just

00:58:16.940 --> 00:58:19.610 line:84%
spinach and good
for you, but it has

00:58:19.610 --> 00:58:25.760 line:84%
a lot of deep personal
moments from individuals

00:58:25.760 --> 00:58:32.220 line:84%
who are so important
of making the change

00:58:32.220 --> 00:58:38.010 line:84%
and the progress that has been
made by teaspoons as opposed

00:58:38.010 --> 00:58:39.840 line:90%
to in greater leaps and bounds.

00:58:44.200 --> 00:58:46.320 line:90%
So in our hive mind, we found--

00:58:46.320 --> 00:58:49.260 line:84%
as I like to call it--
we also had a space

00:58:49.260 --> 00:58:53.700 line:84%
for important
conversations defined

00:58:53.700 --> 00:59:00.300 line:84%
by our unique experience
and privileges

00:59:00.300 --> 00:59:04.255 line:84%
in some spaces and lack of
privilege in some spaces.

00:59:04.255 --> 00:59:09.360 line:84%
Bao, you're student
film, Julian,

00:59:09.360 --> 00:59:13.340 line:90%
won an award at Palm Springs.

00:59:13.340 --> 00:59:15.690 line:84%
Talk to me a little
bit about your journey

00:59:15.690 --> 00:59:19.560 line:84%
to Be Water and how
easy or not easy

00:59:19.560 --> 00:59:27.960 line:84%
it was getting to that place of
making that film and getting--

00:59:27.960 --> 00:59:31.830 line:84%
this is an instance
where a buyer actually

00:59:31.830 --> 00:59:37.050 line:84%
went to someone who's Asian
to actually tell the story.

00:59:37.050 --> 00:59:40.790 line:84%
And talk a little bit
about how that happened.

00:59:40.790 --> 00:59:42.340 line:90%
It was a long time coming.

00:59:42.340 --> 00:59:45.750 line:84%
I mean, as everyone
on the panel knows,

00:59:45.750 --> 00:59:47.760 line:84%
it takes a long time
to make a documentary.

00:59:47.760 --> 00:59:49.350 line:84%
I guess for me,
it was five years,

00:59:49.350 --> 00:59:53.280 line:84%
which isn't too long considered
for many other documentary

00:59:53.280 --> 00:59:53.790 line:90%
filmmakers.

00:59:53.790 --> 00:59:57.090 line:84%
But they try to make a
film about the most famous

00:59:57.090 --> 01:00:00.000 line:84%
Asian-American and
not get any traction,

01:00:00.000 --> 01:00:02.340 line:84%
I thought it was
a bit difficult,

01:00:02.340 --> 01:00:04.290 line:84%
just kind of eye
opening in many ways.

01:00:04.290 --> 01:00:06.840 line:84%
It took five years from
the original conception

01:00:06.840 --> 01:00:10.210 line:84%
to when we finally premiered
this year at Sundance.

01:00:10.210 --> 01:00:13.800 line:84%
And I got a lot of pushback
because there had been so

01:00:13.800 --> 01:00:15.290 line:90%
many Bruce Lee documentaries.

01:00:15.290 --> 01:00:17.310 line:90%
There been like 40.

01:00:17.310 --> 01:00:20.310 line:84%
But surprisingly, or maybe
not surprisingly, there

01:00:20.310 --> 01:00:22.440 line:84%
haven't been any
features directed

01:00:22.440 --> 01:00:25.680 line:84%
by an Asian-American about the
most famous Asian-American.

01:00:25.680 --> 01:00:28.920 line:84%
And I knew that the lens that
I wanted to tell Bruce Lee's

01:00:28.920 --> 01:00:30.090 line:90%
story was different.

01:00:30.090 --> 01:00:31.890 line:84%
It was somewhat
personal and honest,

01:00:31.890 --> 01:00:35.880 line:84%
even though I don't know
Bruce Lee personally.

01:00:35.880 --> 01:00:38.950 line:84%
I knew that my perspective was
something that was different.

01:00:38.950 --> 01:00:43.620 line:84%
And that could lend value
to Bruce's narrative.

01:00:43.620 --> 01:00:47.070 line:90%
And ESPN was fortunate--

01:00:47.070 --> 01:00:49.680 line:84%
I was fortunate enough
to connect with ESPN.

01:00:49.680 --> 01:00:52.270 line:84%
And they allowed me
to tell this story

01:00:52.270 --> 01:00:54.708 line:84%
in, again, unapologetically
Asian-American way.

01:00:54.708 --> 01:00:56.250 line:84%
So I think a lot of
people, when they

01:00:56.250 --> 01:01:00.190 line:84%
think of Bruce Lee, he's sort
of this global cultural icon.

01:01:00.190 --> 01:01:02.340 line:84%
He transcends his
race in so many ways

01:01:02.340 --> 01:01:04.290 line:84%
and he's not seen as
an Asian-American.

01:01:04.290 --> 01:01:07.980 line:84%
So I wanted people to know this
was an Asian-American male,

01:01:07.980 --> 01:01:09.510 line:90%
growing up in--

01:01:09.510 --> 01:01:11.490 line:84%
coming to America
in the 1960s, trying

01:01:11.490 --> 01:01:14.940 line:84%
to make it in Hollywood in 1960s
when the Vietnam War is just

01:01:14.940 --> 01:01:16.200 line:90%
starting to boil up.

01:01:16.200 --> 01:01:19.020 line:84%
The Korean war had
ended a decade earlier.

01:01:19.020 --> 01:01:22.620 line:84%
And then two decades earlier
was the World War II where

01:01:22.620 --> 01:01:24.030 line:90%
the Japanese were in turn.

01:01:24.030 --> 01:01:26.640 line:84%
So the enemy of
America was very much

01:01:26.640 --> 01:01:28.440 line:90%
the face of the Asian-American.

01:01:28.440 --> 01:01:31.890 line:84%
And I thought it was
important to put that

01:01:31.890 --> 01:01:33.250 line:90%
into Bruce Lee's narrative.

01:01:33.250 --> 01:01:34.920 line:90%
It's mythology in so many ways.

01:01:34.920 --> 01:01:40.260 line:84%
Because, again, it's hard
enough to be kind of BIPOC

01:01:40.260 --> 01:01:41.630 line:90%
actor/actress today.

01:01:41.630 --> 01:01:44.730 line:84%
But trying to be in
Hollywood in the 1960s

01:01:44.730 --> 01:01:46.200 line:90%
is seemingly impossible.

01:01:46.200 --> 01:01:48.030 line:84%
But people always think
that Bruce Lee just

01:01:48.030 --> 01:01:50.850 line:84%
was always this huge icon
that he never struggled.

01:01:50.850 --> 01:01:54.150 line:84%
So to kind of understand
and unpack that struggle,

01:01:54.150 --> 01:01:56.880 line:84%
I knew it was important
to see all the moments

01:01:56.880 --> 01:01:59.640 line:84%
in American history where
Asian and Asian-Americans were

01:01:59.640 --> 01:02:00.720 line:90%
disenfranchised.

01:02:00.720 --> 01:02:02.390 line:90%
They were ostracized.

01:02:02.390 --> 01:02:07.740 line:84%
They were felt as
humans, as American.

01:02:07.740 --> 01:02:11.880 line:84%
And again, I was really
fortunate to explore

01:02:11.880 --> 01:02:12.790 line:90%
Bruce Lee's life.

01:02:12.790 --> 01:02:15.690 line:84%
And it was a testament
to his character

01:02:15.690 --> 01:02:18.840 line:84%
that he didn't come to
America trying to assimilate.

01:02:18.840 --> 01:02:21.360 line:84%
He came to America
knowing that, I learned

01:02:21.360 --> 01:02:23.430 line:90%
martial arts from Hong Kong.

01:02:23.430 --> 01:02:25.740 line:84%
I'm going to bring that
side of me to America,

01:02:25.740 --> 01:02:28.410 line:84%
and that's how I'm going
to become American.

01:02:28.410 --> 01:02:30.810 line:84%
It's that you're going to
accept the best side of me,

01:02:30.810 --> 01:02:33.600 line:84%
and I'm going to mold that
into what the best of America

01:02:33.600 --> 01:02:37.740 line:84%
can be instead of trying to
assimilate into everyone else's

01:02:37.740 --> 01:02:38.593 line:90%
idea of America

01:02:38.593 --> 01:02:40.260 line:84%
I think that's a
conversation that we're

01:02:40.260 --> 01:02:41.400 line:90%
having today in many ways.

01:02:41.400 --> 01:02:44.040 line:84%
It's like are we
bringing ourselves

01:02:44.040 --> 01:02:50.490 line:84%
by becoming these diverse voices
in a roomful of familiar faces?

01:02:50.490 --> 01:02:53.350 line:84%
Or are we being
ourselves an American

01:02:53.350 --> 01:02:57.920 line:84%
by bringing the best of our
authenticity, our voice,

01:02:57.920 --> 01:03:00.390 line:84%
until what the future
of America will become?

01:03:00.390 --> 01:03:05.530 line:84%
And again, I've learned so much
listening to everyone today.

01:03:05.530 --> 01:03:07.890 line:90%
And we all have to listen more.

01:03:07.890 --> 01:03:09.010 line:90%
We all have to learn more.

01:03:09.010 --> 01:03:11.430 line:84%
And I think that's
something we can take away

01:03:11.430 --> 01:03:13.050 line:90%
in the future for sure.

01:03:13.050 --> 01:03:14.670 line:90%
Absolutely.

01:03:14.670 --> 01:03:17.430 line:90%
Beautifully said.

01:03:17.430 --> 01:03:21.630 line:90%
Well, as we sort of wrap up.

01:03:21.630 --> 01:03:26.160 line:84%
I'd love to just
hear from everyone.

01:03:26.160 --> 01:03:27.270 line:90%
Where do we go from here?

01:03:30.370 --> 01:03:35.550 line:84%
When I look out at
this panel, all of you

01:03:35.550 --> 01:03:41.310 line:84%
are authentically telling your
story from your community,

01:03:41.310 --> 01:03:42.760 line:90%
from your perspective.

01:03:42.760 --> 01:03:44.630 line:90%
And that's a beautiful thing.

01:03:44.630 --> 01:03:50.930 line:84%
But we know that that's not the
case in the industry at large.

01:03:50.930 --> 01:03:58.880 line:90%
So where do we go from here?

01:03:58.880 --> 01:04:00.660 line:90%
Who does the work?

01:04:00.660 --> 01:04:06.410 line:84%
How do we sort of take it
beyond the symbolic diversity

01:04:06.410 --> 01:04:09.050 line:84%
in this corporate
strategy, as Bao

01:04:09.050 --> 01:04:13.665 line:84%
says, of diversity initiatives
to some actual real concrete

01:04:13.665 --> 01:04:14.165 line:90%
change?

01:04:16.720 --> 01:04:22.450 line:84%
I'll just go around, and I will
start with you Lisa Cortés.

01:04:22.450 --> 01:04:24.580 line:90%
Wow, Roger.

01:04:24.580 --> 01:04:31.310 line:84%
Well, listen I-- we want
narratives sovereignty.

01:04:31.310 --> 01:04:39.600 line:84%
We want to that ability
to have unfettered access

01:04:39.600 --> 01:04:45.900 line:84%
to opportunity, to
telling the stories that

01:04:45.900 --> 01:04:52.560 line:84%
are unique from our perspective,
and to receiving distribution

01:04:52.560 --> 01:04:53.670 line:90%
for those stories.

01:04:53.670 --> 01:04:55.050 line:90%
I mean, it's one thing to tell.

01:04:55.050 --> 01:04:59.850 line:84%
It's a whole other conversation
that we haven't had

01:04:59.850 --> 01:05:02.445 line:90%
about the access to the buyers.

01:05:08.460 --> 01:05:15.740 line:84%
So in that, in
this moment, as we

01:05:15.740 --> 01:05:20.650 line:84%
move towards the
sovereignty, I would

01:05:20.650 --> 01:05:24.910 line:84%
ask those who were
former allies but are now

01:05:24.910 --> 01:05:26.800 line:90%
in solidarity with us--

01:05:26.800 --> 01:05:37.220 line:84%
thank you, Bao-- to
step up and to join us.

01:05:37.220 --> 01:05:40.580 line:84%
Because we can expand
the scope of the stories

01:05:40.580 --> 01:05:45.175 line:84%
that are out there
by working together.

01:05:48.120 --> 01:05:49.480 line:90%
Hear, hear.

01:05:49.480 --> 01:05:49.980 line:90%
Jim?

01:05:52.920 --> 01:05:58.110 line:84%
I think that there are
many barriers for people

01:05:58.110 --> 01:06:02.760 line:84%
with disabilities or who
are deaf or hard of hearing

01:06:02.760 --> 01:06:06.140 line:90%
to really have a career.

01:06:06.140 --> 01:06:09.060 line:84%
And one of the things that I've
always thought about is that

01:06:09.060 --> 01:06:10.950 line:90%
if you don't--

01:06:10.950 --> 01:06:14.790 line:84%
can you imagine me working if
you have never seen somebody

01:06:14.790 --> 01:06:18.410 line:90%
like me working?

01:06:18.410 --> 01:06:21.020 line:84%
So we have to kind of
prime the pump here.

01:06:21.020 --> 01:06:25.910 line:84%
We have to look at how
people enter the business.

01:06:25.910 --> 01:06:30.310 line:84%
And if you're judging
someone's commitment or ability

01:06:30.310 --> 01:06:33.070 line:84%
by whether they can
work a 20-hour day

01:06:33.070 --> 01:06:37.200 line:84%
or can carry a tray full
of coffee for people,

01:06:37.200 --> 01:06:39.310 line:84%
you're filtering
out a lot of people

01:06:39.310 --> 01:06:45.410 line:84%
whose experiences and
stories we're missing out on.

01:06:45.410 --> 01:06:48.160 line:84%
And so you have to
look at how you're

01:06:48.160 --> 01:06:51.040 line:84%
judging someone's
worth and someone's

01:06:51.040 --> 01:06:53.530 line:90%
value and their talent.

01:06:53.530 --> 01:06:57.810 line:84%
And it's not that hard
to integrate people

01:06:57.810 --> 01:06:59.610 line:90%
with disabilities into this.

01:06:59.610 --> 01:07:01.980 line:84%
You just have to look
at how other people have

01:07:01.980 --> 01:07:03.570 line:90%
made their career.

01:07:03.570 --> 01:07:07.120 line:84%
And if I'm not able to
work in the mailroom,

01:07:07.120 --> 01:07:10.660 line:84%
doesn't mean that I don't
deserve to be a director

01:07:10.660 --> 01:07:14.620 line:90%
or run a motion picture studio.

01:07:14.620 --> 01:07:21.315 line:84%
And finally, we talk
about inclusion and such.

01:07:21.315 --> 01:07:22.690 line:84%
And what's really
important to me

01:07:22.690 --> 01:07:25.490 line:84%
as somebody with
a mobility issue

01:07:25.490 --> 01:07:31.220 line:84%
is that I want to see
somebody with a disability,

01:07:31.220 --> 01:07:36.200 line:84%
if they won an award to get an
Oscar an Emmy or anything else,

01:07:36.200 --> 01:07:40.730 line:84%
I want us to be entering the
stage exactly the same way

01:07:40.730 --> 01:07:43.390 line:90%
as everybody else does.

01:07:43.390 --> 01:07:48.130 line:84%
None of this waiting in the
wings, or "it's too difficult,"

01:07:48.130 --> 01:07:52.060 line:84%
but that we accept the
fact that if you really

01:07:52.060 --> 01:07:57.310 line:84%
believe in inclusion,
that we also get our time

01:07:57.310 --> 01:08:01.910 line:84%
to enter the stage
not differently

01:08:01.910 --> 01:08:03.500 line:84%
but the same way
everybody else does.

01:08:07.890 --> 01:08:10.110 line:90%
I'm so glad you said that Jim.

01:08:10.110 --> 01:08:13.680 line:84%
Because when we won
the Oscar, Prudence

01:08:13.680 --> 01:08:14.910 line:90%
couldn't come on stage.

01:08:14.910 --> 01:08:16.830 line:84%
Because they said
there was no way

01:08:16.830 --> 01:08:21.390 line:84%
to get her wheelchair onto that
stage at the Kodak Theater.

01:08:21.390 --> 01:08:25.000 line:84%
And it was they were like,
we'll just cut away to her.

01:08:25.000 --> 01:08:27.790 line:90%
And it was devastating.

01:08:27.790 --> 01:08:29.069 line:90%
I mean it was for her.

01:08:29.069 --> 01:08:31.170 line:90%
She didn't have that moment.

01:08:31.170 --> 01:08:34.100 line:84%
Because it wasn't
wheelchair-friendly.

01:08:34.100 --> 01:08:36.569 line:84%
Like, it takes too long,
it was too complicated.

01:08:36.569 --> 01:08:38.819 line:84%
It was too-- it was
just a bunch of excuses

01:08:38.819 --> 01:08:40.770 line:84%
why she couldn't
be on that stage.

01:08:40.770 --> 01:08:43.800 line:84%
And I fought and argued
and fought and they still--

01:08:43.800 --> 01:08:45.960 line:90%
they just wouldn't budge.

01:08:45.960 --> 01:08:49.470 line:84%
Well, I think that it's
important from the red carpet I

01:08:49.470 --> 01:08:51.990 line:90%
see that flight of stairs.

01:08:51.990 --> 01:08:56.420 line:84%
And I'm thinking, why is
there a flight of stairs?

01:08:56.420 --> 01:08:58.910 line:84%
Well, when the
Kodak was designed

01:08:58.910 --> 01:09:01.580 line:84%
or anything else was
done, they didn't really

01:09:01.580 --> 01:09:04.890 line:84%
think about accommodating
people like myself

01:09:04.890 --> 01:09:07.880 line:84%
exactly in the same
league as everybody else.

01:09:07.880 --> 01:09:13.479 line:84%
Or we can do something special,
or we got a lift off there.

01:09:13.479 --> 01:09:16.779 line:84%
True inclusion means
that I get to hold

01:09:16.779 --> 01:09:21.990 line:84%
the hand of my co-director
and co-producer

01:09:21.990 --> 01:09:25.420 line:84%
going up on that stage together
the same way everybody else

01:09:25.420 --> 01:09:27.890 line:90%
does.

01:09:27.890 --> 01:09:29.029 line:90%
Absolutely.

01:09:29.029 --> 01:09:29.830 line:90%
Absolutely.

01:09:29.830 --> 01:09:31.220 line:90%
Bao?

01:09:31.220 --> 01:09:31.770 line:90%
Yeah.

01:09:31.770 --> 01:09:34.479 line:84%
I mean I think, again,
having conversations

01:09:34.479 --> 01:09:35.950 line:90%
like these are important.

01:09:35.950 --> 01:09:39.910 line:84%
I think people in power,
people in the industry

01:09:39.910 --> 01:09:43.210 line:84%
can benefit from listening
more and not necessarily

01:09:43.210 --> 01:09:45.370 line:84%
be defensive when we're
having these uncomfortable

01:09:45.370 --> 01:09:46.180 line:90%
conversations.

01:09:46.180 --> 01:09:50.020 line:84%
Because I think
for us, as BIPOC,

01:09:50.020 --> 01:09:52.930 line:84%
as marginalized
filmmakers in some, ways

01:09:52.930 --> 01:09:56.890 line:84%
we've been feeling this level
of discomfort in our daily lives

01:09:56.890 --> 01:09:57.940 line:90%
for so long.

01:09:57.940 --> 01:10:01.810 line:84%
And it's time to-- everyone can
feel a little uncomfortable.

01:10:01.810 --> 01:10:04.480 line:84%
I think immediate
action is knowing,

01:10:04.480 --> 01:10:08.980 line:84%
recognizing that you creating
a diversity initiative,

01:10:08.980 --> 01:10:10.630 line:90%
is it something that's new?

01:10:10.630 --> 01:10:14.200 line:84%
There's been organizations
that have been doing this work

01:10:14.200 --> 01:10:18.590 line:84%
and making space and lanes
for communities for decades.

01:10:18.590 --> 01:10:23.650 line:84%
The Center for Asian American
Media, Third World Newsreel,

01:10:23.650 --> 01:10:27.400 line:84%
now today with Firelight,
with [? ADOC, ?] Visual

01:10:27.400 --> 01:10:29.977 line:84%
Communications, Brown
Girls Doc Mafia--

01:10:29.977 --> 01:10:31.060 line:90%
support these communities.

01:10:31.060 --> 01:10:33.230 line:90%
Listen to these organizations.

01:10:33.230 --> 01:10:36.400 line:84%
And that's something
that's very immediate.

01:10:36.400 --> 01:10:40.630 line:84%
And another thing is
allowing more than one story

01:10:40.630 --> 01:10:45.250 line:84%
to be told about something,
understanding that there's

01:10:45.250 --> 01:10:46.690 line:90%
a danger in the single story.

01:10:46.690 --> 01:10:49.300 line:84%
That there doesn't have to
be one Vietnam War film.

01:10:49.300 --> 01:10:52.480 line:84%
There doesn't have to be
one Asian-American film

01:10:52.480 --> 01:10:53.350 line:90%
about this subject.

01:10:53.350 --> 01:10:55.670 line:84%
There's so many different
ways to tell a story.

01:10:55.670 --> 01:10:58.270 line:84%
And I encourage people to
make as many Bruce Lee films

01:10:58.270 --> 01:10:59.422 line:90%
as possible.

01:10:59.422 --> 01:11:01.630 line:84%
I think sometimes people
think like documentaries are

01:11:01.630 --> 01:11:03.520 line:90%
definitive, historical records.

01:11:03.520 --> 01:11:05.000 line:90%
And no.

01:11:05.000 --> 01:11:06.210 line:90%
They're valuable.

01:11:06.210 --> 01:11:08.680 line:84%
They're very
subjective sometimes.

01:11:08.680 --> 01:11:12.580 line:84%
And I think there's always ways
to learn more from these spaces

01:11:12.580 --> 01:11:16.510 line:90%
And in any--

01:11:16.510 --> 01:11:20.800 line:84%
not just in our industry
and our society,

01:11:20.800 --> 01:11:23.050 line:84%
there's so many ways
that people can help.

01:11:23.050 --> 01:11:25.960 line:84%
Because I think I don't want to
put self-importance onto what

01:11:25.960 --> 01:11:27.430 line:90%
we do as filmmakers.

01:11:27.430 --> 01:11:31.180 line:84%
But I remember watching
your film, Jim,

01:11:31.180 --> 01:11:36.190 line:84%
[? subject Denise ?] saying,
laws won't change attitudes.

01:11:36.190 --> 01:11:40.240 line:84%
And attitudes need to change
through culture, through film,

01:11:40.240 --> 01:11:43.840 line:84%
through books, through the way
we see each other in the world,

01:11:43.840 --> 01:11:46.190 line:84%
through the way we
absorb each other.

01:11:46.190 --> 01:11:50.150 line:84%
And so I think it's important
that in every industry

01:11:50.150 --> 01:11:52.420 line:84%
we work in, we think about
changing those attitudes.

01:11:52.420 --> 01:11:53.960 line:90%
And then laws can change.

01:11:53.960 --> 01:11:55.870 line:90%
And policies can change.

01:11:55.870 --> 01:11:58.030 line:90%
So as an industry, too.

01:11:58.030 --> 01:12:00.390 line:84%
I don't want to think of
this type of conversation

01:12:00.390 --> 01:12:03.580 line:84%
and these initiatives and
this reckoning as a trend

01:12:03.580 --> 01:12:04.900 line:90%
but more as a movement.

01:12:04.900 --> 01:12:10.940 line:84%
Because us existing is an
inevitability in so many ways.

01:12:10.940 --> 01:12:11.870 line:90%
Absolutely.

01:12:11.870 --> 01:12:13.720 line:90%
Absolutely.

01:12:13.720 --> 01:12:16.070 line:90%
Dawn, the elder statesman--

01:12:16.070 --> 01:12:18.800 line:84%
stateswoman, sorry--
elder stateswoman,

01:12:18.800 --> 01:12:22.220 line:90%
you bring it on home.

01:12:22.220 --> 01:12:27.830 line:84%
I just thank all of you for
this conversation today.

01:12:27.830 --> 01:12:29.900 line:90%
It gave me a lot to think about.

01:12:29.900 --> 01:12:33.380 line:84%
I want to encourage
particularly-- well,

01:12:33.380 --> 01:12:35.210 line:84%
I would encourage
everyone to remember

01:12:35.210 --> 01:12:38.220 line:90%
that one size does not fit all.

01:12:38.220 --> 01:12:40.430 line:84%
And so people need
different types

01:12:40.430 --> 01:12:45.230 line:90%
of not just support but respect.

01:12:45.230 --> 01:12:50.930 line:84%
So as Jim says, it's not only
people with disabilities.

01:12:50.930 --> 01:12:55.040 line:84%
But people who are not of means
cannot afford to volunteer

01:12:55.040 --> 01:12:56.660 line:90%
and be interns.

01:12:56.660 --> 01:13:00.470 line:84%
And so you can't rely
on a path of plucking

01:13:00.470 --> 01:13:02.330 line:84%
talented interns
who are supported

01:13:02.330 --> 01:13:05.510 line:84%
by wealthy parents
for your talent pool.

01:13:05.510 --> 01:13:10.460 line:84%
So at the stage of who's
entering, making it possible,

01:13:10.460 --> 01:13:14.000 line:84%
or just thinking about
barriers to entry,

01:13:14.000 --> 01:13:17.090 line:84%
just having that thought,
just thinking about that--

01:13:17.090 --> 01:13:19.700 line:84%
but then I think at
our level, I would

01:13:19.700 --> 01:13:25.350 line:84%
encourage people to think about
how you treat each director.

01:13:25.350 --> 01:13:26.690 line:90%
I mean, I have been--

01:13:26.690 --> 01:13:29.600 line:84%
not by everybody--
but most people,

01:13:29.600 --> 01:13:31.610 line:84%
I have really good
relationships.

01:13:31.610 --> 01:13:35.980 line:84%
But on a few occasions I've
been called very aggressive.

01:13:35.980 --> 01:13:43.490 line:84%
I have been called out for
basically loud, Black woman

01:13:43.490 --> 01:13:47.420 line:84%
behavior in situations
where I was, I think,

01:13:47.420 --> 01:13:50.120 line:90%
being a leader of a team.

01:13:50.120 --> 01:13:53.720 line:84%
And so I kind of
challenge people

01:13:53.720 --> 01:14:01.420 line:84%
to think about what assumptions
you bring to your partners,

01:14:01.420 --> 01:14:06.860 line:84%
and understand, I'm
really good at my job.

01:14:06.860 --> 01:14:09.950 line:84%
And if there's something
you're concerned about,

01:14:09.950 --> 01:14:12.470 line:84%
you should tell
it to me straight.

01:14:12.470 --> 01:14:15.030 line:84%
Don't try to hire
a camera around me.

01:14:15.030 --> 01:14:18.380 line:84%
Don't ask for their resume
is of the cinematographers

01:14:18.380 --> 01:14:20.810 line:84%
or producers I bring
to double check

01:14:20.810 --> 01:14:23.810 line:90%
whether they're qualified.

01:14:23.810 --> 01:14:27.050 line:90%
So there are a lot of--

01:14:27.050 --> 01:14:29.750 line:84%
I don't know if I would
call them microaggressions,

01:14:29.750 --> 01:14:36.650 line:84%
but there's cracks around these
seams where you know that as--

01:14:39.230 --> 01:14:41.180 line:90%
my career's great right now.

01:14:41.180 --> 01:14:45.330 line:84%
And I am just thrilled
at the opportunity.

01:14:45.330 --> 01:14:48.140 line:84%
And I'm running after as
many as I can while also

01:14:48.140 --> 01:14:50.720 line:90%
trying to make space.

01:14:50.720 --> 01:14:55.610 line:84%
But when-- it's exhausting
to demand respect

01:14:55.610 --> 01:14:58.710 line:90%
from people at this stage.

01:14:58.710 --> 01:15:04.700 line:84%
And so I just encourage you to
put yourselves in our shoes.

01:15:04.700 --> 01:15:09.690 line:84%
I think empathy is a
great skill builder.

01:15:09.690 --> 01:15:13.250 line:84%
So do what you need
to do to have empathy.

01:15:16.040 --> 01:15:23.630 line:84%
I've been giving a lot of
executives a reading list.

01:15:23.630 --> 01:15:27.410 line:84%
And I've been-- at the top
of that reading list has

01:15:27.410 --> 01:15:31.190 line:84%
been Ibram X. Kendi's How
to Be an Antiracist, which

01:15:31.190 --> 01:15:33.860 line:84%
is the number one book on the
New York Times bestseller list.

01:15:33.860 --> 01:15:37.880 line:84%
Because white people
are reading it.

01:15:37.880 --> 01:15:44.930 line:84%
And they're like oh, OK, I have
to actually be anti-racist.

01:15:44.930 --> 01:15:47.615 line:84%
I can't just sort of
just not be racist.

01:15:52.730 --> 01:15:57.170 line:84%
And I tend-- I give it to
executives, because they're

01:15:57.170 --> 01:16:01.130 line:84%
the ones who are going to
be having to hire people

01:16:01.130 --> 01:16:02.880 line:90%
that don't look like them.

01:16:02.880 --> 01:16:05.270 line:84%
They're the ones who
are going to be--

01:16:05.270 --> 01:16:07.910 line:84%
who are going to empower those
people to commission films.

01:16:11.570 --> 01:16:14.210 line:84%
But I don't want-- and
I'm like the work is not--

01:16:14.210 --> 01:16:15.590 line:90%
I can't do all the work.

01:16:15.590 --> 01:16:19.200 line:84%
You have to-- you
have to do the work.

01:16:19.200 --> 01:16:22.637 line:84%
You have to be an
anti-racist, or the world's

01:16:22.637 --> 01:16:23.720 line:90%
going to leave you behind.

01:16:23.720 --> 01:16:25.340 line:90%
Because we're moving forward.

01:16:25.340 --> 01:16:28.280 line:90%
We're moving forward regardless.

01:16:28.280 --> 01:16:31.880 line:84%
And nothing is going to
stop progress in the world.

01:16:31.880 --> 01:16:33.220 line:90%
It may be slowed down.

01:16:33.220 --> 01:16:36.320 line:84%
It may be temporary
sort of scary moments,

01:16:36.320 --> 01:16:39.200 line:84%
but that's because
progress is happening.

01:16:39.200 --> 01:16:40.580 line:84%
And I think progress
is happening

01:16:40.580 --> 01:16:43.220 line:90%
in the documentary community.

01:16:43.220 --> 01:16:45.830 line:90%
And it's very exciting.

01:16:45.830 --> 01:16:48.290 line:84%
And it's evidenced
by all of you,

01:16:48.290 --> 01:16:50.900 line:84%
and who you are, and
the films you've made,

01:16:50.900 --> 01:16:52.280 line:90%
and what you've done.

01:16:52.280 --> 01:16:57.930 line:84%
All of you just make me so
excited to be in this business.

01:16:57.930 --> 01:17:00.560 line:84%
I'm so proud of each and every
one of your films and all

01:17:00.560 --> 01:17:01.580 line:90%
your work.

01:17:01.580 --> 01:17:08.730 line:84%
And I look forward to seeing
your work for tens of--

01:17:08.730 --> 01:17:10.387 line:84%
to the future--
all the work you're

01:17:10.387 --> 01:17:11.470 line:90%
going to do in the future.

01:17:11.470 --> 01:17:17.490 line:84%
So thank you all for
being part of the Academy,

01:17:17.490 --> 01:17:20.610 line:84%
for the work you're
doing in the Academy

01:17:20.610 --> 01:17:25.140 line:84%
to move it forward, to move
it out of the Dark Ages

01:17:25.140 --> 01:17:28.110 line:90%
into the light.

01:17:28.110 --> 01:17:29.760 line:90%
Thank you.

01:17:29.760 --> 01:17:32.220 line:84%
And then for those
of you who are--

01:17:32.220 --> 01:17:34.930 line:84%
Bao, you will be
an Academy member.

01:17:34.930 --> 01:17:36.000 line:90%
I have no doubt.

01:17:36.000 --> 01:17:39.170 line:90%
Your work is spectacular.

01:17:39.170 --> 01:17:41.820 line:90%
This panel has been great.

01:17:41.820 --> 01:17:46.530 line:84%
And thank you all for
watching Academy Dialogues.

01:17:46.530 --> 01:17:48.210 line:84%
Check out our
previous discussions

01:17:48.210 --> 01:17:52.340 line:84%
and subscribe to the Oscar
channel for more videos.

